(320919,'Hughes, John (I)','John Wilden Hughes Jr. \n18 February 1950, Lansing, Michigan, USA \n6 August 2009, Manhattan, New York City, New York, USA (heart attack) \n6\' 1\" \n\n* \'Nancy Ludwig\' (1970 - 6 August 2009) (his death); 2 children \n\n* Movies often take place in Shermer, Illinois \n* Sets films in Chicago area \n* Most of his teen movies from the 1980s have a scene in the school lunchroom, school library, or both. \n* References to art - characters discussing art or posed to resemble works of art \n* Close-ups of two characters locking eyes with each other \n* Many of his 1980s Teen films have the same name as song titles: _Sixteen Candles (1984)_ (qv) (Stray Cats), _Pretty in Pink (1986)_ (qv) (\'The Psychedelic Furs\' (qv)), _She\'s Having a Baby (1988)_ (qv) (\'Dave Wakeling\' (qv)) which are also heard in the film. \n* His movies usually end with a freeze-frame \n* Often has one character silently mimicking another character as the line is spoken, as if it\'s been said before. \n* Characters who are either stuck in one place or traveling somewhere \n* Often has a scene-stealing and usually dotty supporting character which serves little use for the actual plot (for example: Long Duk Dong in _Sixteen Candles (1984)_ (qv) and Chet in _Weird Science (1985)_ (qv)). \n* Often cast members of the \"Brat Pack\" \n* Portrayal of Yuppie life \n* Often has a character use the pejorative term \"four-flusher\", a term originating in poker which means one who bluffs or boasts. \n* Often worked with \'John Candy\' (qv) \n* Often uses freeze frames at the end of his films. \n* Automobiles play prominent and even character-defining roles in several John Hughes films. There\'s the bizarre station wagon in \"National Lampoon\'s Vacation\" (1983); the Rolls Royce in \"Sixteen Candles\" (1984); the various cars indicating the social status of the kids on detention in \"The Breakfast Club\" (1985) (a BMW for the moneyed prom queen, an SUV for the jock); the Ferrari in \"Ferris Bueller\'s Day Off\" (1986); and the Marathon Car Rental scene in \"Planes, Trains & Automobiles\" (1987). \n\n* (1968) Graduated Glenbrook North High School, Northbrook, Illinois, USA. \n* Started out in the 1970s as a writer for National Lampoon magazine. \n* A number of his movies have a small scene after the credits. \n* One reason that John Hughes films are set in Shermer, Illinois, is because Northbrook was once called Shermerville. Furthermore, Glenbrook North High School is located on Shermer Road. \n* Was a fan of \'The Beatles\' (qv) and frequently had characters in his movies sing Beatles songs: \'Anthony Michael Hall\' (qv) (the Geek) sings \"Hey Jude\" in _Sixteen Candles (1984)_ (qv), \'Jon Cryer\' (qv) (Duckie) sings \"Love\" in _Pretty in Pink (1986)_ (qv), and \'Matthew Broderick (I)\' (qv) (Ferris Bueller) sings \"Twist & Shout\" in _Ferris Bueller\'s Day Off (1986)_ (qv). \n* Used the pen name Edmond Dantès, a homage to the lead character in _The Count of Monte Cristo (1934)_ (qv). \n* Wrote roles for \'John Candy\' (qv), \'Anthony Michael Hall\' (qv), and \'Molly Ringwald\' (qv). \n* Inspired the \"Brat Pack\" movement of the 1980s. \n* He wrote two movies where the main character is involved in a race with the theme from _Chariots of Fire (1981)_ (qv) going on in the background (_Mr. Mom (1983)_ (qv) and _National Lampoon\'s Vacation (1983)_ (qv)). \n* Lived, for some time, in Williams Bay, Wisconsin. \n* Although he was offered the distinguished alumni award from Glenbrook North High School, he refused it in reflection of his not so great memories of the north shore and the school itself. \n* Father of writer/producer \'James Hughes (I)\' (qv) and musician \'John Hughes (IX)\' (qv). \n* Owned farm land in Harvard, Illinois. (2008). \n* The premiere episode of _\"Community\" (2009)_ (qv) (\"Pilot\") is dedicated to his memory. \n* Attended Arizona State University. \n* Rarely gave interviews. By the 1990\'s, shunned publicity completely, withdrawing from public life. \n* When casting the role of Kevin McCallister in _Home Alone (1990)_ (qv), \'Chris Columbus\' (qv) had already seen \'Macaulay Culkin\' (qv) in _Uncle Buck (1989)_ (qv) but he wanted to audition some other kids while Hughes had his heart set on Culkin. So Columbus met Culkin in New York and was very charmed by him and thought he was fantastic. Columbus auditioned five other kids and none of them shaped up to Culkin. Columbus went with Culkin because he was not as picture perfect and he had an instant relatability to the kids in the audience. Columbus knew the cameras would love him and he was immensely funny. \n* \'Chris Columbus\' (qv) was sent two scripts by Hughes while he was staying with his wife\'s parents in Chicago after the birth of their first child; one was for _Home Alone (1990)_ (qv). Columbus fell in love with it, it really struck a chord with him and he felt he could make a really strong film out of it. He and Hughes hit it off instantly so Hughes gave him the job to direct. \n* Two of Hughes\' films have been selected for preservation by the National Film Registry of the Library of Congress. \"Ferris Bueller\'s Day Off\" was chosen in 2014, and \"The Breakfast Club\" in 2016. \n* Hughes notoriously cut off contact with his favorite young performers - \'Molly Ringwald\' (qv) and \'Anthony Michael Hall\' (qv) - after they rejected some of his scripts in the mid-1980s. When he died in 2009, Hughes hadn\'t spoken to either of them in over 20 years. In a NY Times editorial about his death, Ringwald wrote: \"Most people who knew John knew that he was able to hold a grudge longer than anyone -- his grudges were almost supernatural things, enduring for years, even decades. [Hall] suspects that he was never forgiven for turning down parts in \"Pretty in Pink\" and \"Ferris Bueller\'s Day Off\". I turned down later films as well [\"Some Kind of Wonderful\"]. Not because I didn\'t want to work with John anymore -- I loved working with him, more than anyone before or since\". \n* Hughes died of a sudden heart attack while taking a morning stroll down West 55th Street in Manhattan. At the time he was visiting family in New York City. \n* Many examples of unproduced screenplays he had wrote and were rejected by studios were:  National Lampoon\'s Jaws 3/People 0 (1979). A documentary spoof of the making of Jaws. Due to a lawsuit from Steven Spielberg looming if it was made, Universal Studios scrapped it.  National Lampoon\'s The Joy of Sex (1981). A comedy anthology film about the life of a young man obsessed with sex, even when he enters adulthood. It was originally to star John Belushi and be directed by Penny Marshall, but when a reluctant Belushi decided to go ahead with the film, he died from a drug overdose the next morning and Paramount did not produce the movie. The script was eventually re-written as a teenage sex comedy under the title Joy of Sex in 1984. Matty Simmons was involved with the movie, but ordered to have his name and the National Lampoon moniker taken off the film after he saw the final cut.  The New Kid (1985). A movie meant to star Anthony Michael Hall as the new kid in college.  Oil and Vinegar (1987). A comedy-drama road movie meant to star Matthew Broderick and Ally Sheedy. Broderick would play a groom driving out of town to a wedding with Sheedy as a hitchhiker that he picks up. They both then talk about their personal problems in the car for the remainder of the trip.  Bartholomew Vs. Neff (1991). A comedy meant to star John Candy and Sylvester Stallone as feuding neighbors.  Tickets (1997). A group of teenagers pull an all-nighter waiting for tickets to a legendary rock concert.  Grisbys Go Broke (2003). A middle-class family lose all their money and are forced to spend the holidays frugally. It was rumored that Paramount was going to make this movie upon the passing of Hughes, but this has yet to be determined. \n* While filming Planes, Trains & Automobiles actors John Candy & Steve Martin shared a drink with John Hughes at Naperville, Illinois\' The Lantern Tavern & Grill. \n\n* Audio commentary for the film _Ferris Bueller\'s Day Off (1986)_ (qv). \n\n* \'Kirk Honeycutt\' (qv). _John Hughes: A Life in Film: The Genius Behind Ferris Bueller, The Breakfast Club, Home Alone, and more._ New York: Race Point Publishing, 2015. ISBN 1631060228 \n\n* I stumbled into this business, I didn\'t train for it. I yelled \"Action!\" on my first two movies before the camera was turned on. \n* I don\'t think of kids as a lower form of the human species. \n\n* \"The Independent: Independent Life\" (UK), 11 August 2009, Iss. 7122, pg. 2 - 5, by: Various, \"The John Hughes Club: Personal tributes to Cinema\'s high priest of adolescence\" \n* \"The Independent\" (UK), 8 August 2009, Iss. 7120, pg. 42, by: John Riley, \"Obituary: John Hughes - Writer, director and producer whose Eighties teen movies captured a generation on film\" \n* \"The Washington Post\" (USA), 8 August 2009, Vol. 132, Iss. 246, pg. C1+C8, by: Hank Stuever, \"Most Likely to Understand: John Hughes Knew Our Secret: No One Fit In in High School\" \n* \"The Times\" (UK), 8 August 2009, by: Anonymous, \"John Hughes: film director of The Breakfast Club\" \n* \"Shock Cinema\" (USA), October 1999, Iss. 15, pg. 30, by: Howard S. Berger, \"\"A Trio of Sadistic Holiday Treats\"\"'),
(359466,'Keaton, Michael','Michael John Douglas \nMichael Douglas Keats Dougie \n5 September 1951, Coraopolis, Pennsylvania, USA \n5\' 9\" \nQuirky, inventive and handsome American actor Michael Keaton first achieved major fame with his door-busting performance as fast-talking ideas man Bill Blazejowski, alongside a nerdish morgue attendant (\'Henry Winkler\' (qv)), in _Night Shift (1982)_ (qv). He played further comedic roles in _Mr. Mom (1983)_ (qv), _Johnny Dangerously (1984)_ (qv), and _Beetlejuice (1988)_ (qv), earned further acclaim for his dramatic portrayal of Bruce Wayne / Batman in \'Tim Burton (I)\' (qv)\'s _Batman (1989)_ (qv) and _Batman Returns (1992)_ (qv), and since then, has moved easily between film genres, ranging from drama and romantic comedy to thriller and action.  Keaton was born Michael John Douglas on September 5, 1951 in Coraopolis, Pennsylvania, to Leona Elizabeth (Loftus), a homemaker, and George A. Douglas, a civil engineer and surveyor. He is of Irish, as well as English, Scottish, and German, descent. Michael studied speech for two years at Kent State, before dropping out and moving to Pittsburgh. An unsuccessful attempt at stand-up comedy led Keaton to working as a TV cameraman in a cable station, and he came to realize he wanted to work in front of the cameras. Keaton first appeared on TV in several episodes of _\"MisteRogers\' Neighborhood\" (1968)_ (qv).  He left Pittsburgh and moved to Los Angeles to begin auditioning for TV. He began cropping up in popular TV shows including _\"Maude\" (1972)_ (qv) and _\"The Mary Tyler Moore Hour\" (1979)_ (qv). Around this time, Keaton decided to use an alternative surname to remove confusion with better-known actor \'Michael Douglas (I)\' (qv). After reading an article on actress \'Diane Keaton\' (qv), he decided that Michael Keaton sounded good. His next break was scoring a co-starring role alongside \'Jim Belushi\' (qv) in the short-lived comedy series _\"Working Stiffs\" (1979)_ (qv), which showcased his comedic talent and led to his co-starring role in _Night Shift (1982)_ (qv). Keaton next scored the lead in the comedy hits _Mr. Mom (1983)_ (qv), _Johnny Dangerously (1984)_ (qv) , _Gung Ho (1986)_ (qv), the \'Tim Burton (I)\' (qv) horror-comedy _Beetlejuice (1988)_ (qv), and _The Dream Team (1989)_ (qv).  Keaton\'s career was given another major boost when he was again cast by \'Tim Burton (I)\' (qv), this time as the title comic book superhero, millionaire playboy / crime-fighter Bruce Wayne, in _Batman (1989)_ (qv). Burton cast him because he thought that Keaton was the only actor who could portray someone who has the kind of darkly obsessive personality that the character demands. To say there were howls of protest by fans of the caped crusader comic strip is an understatement! Warner Bros. was deluged with thousands of letters of complaint commenting that comedian Keaton was the wrong choice for the Caped Crusader, given his prior work and the fact that he lacked the suave, handsome features and tall, muscular physicality often attributed to the character in the comic books. However, their fears were proven wrong when Keaton turned in a sensational performance, and he held his own on screen with opponent \'Jack Nicholson (I)\' (qv), playing the lunatic villain, \"The Joker\". Keaton\'s dramatic work earned widespread acclaim from critics and audiences alike, and _Batman (1989)_ (qv) became one of the most successful films of the year.  Keaton remained active during the 1990s, appearing in a wide range of films. Keen to diversify his work, Keaton starred as a psychotic tenant in _Pacific Heights (1990)_ (qv), as a hard-working cop in _One Good Cop (1991)_ (qv), and then donned the black cape and cowl once more for _Batman Returns (1992)_ (qv). He remained in demand during the 1990s, appearing in a wide range of films, including the star-studded Shakespearian _Much Ado About Nothing (1993)_ (qv), the drama _My Life (1993)_ (qv), another \'Ron Howard (I)\' (qv) comedy _The Paper (1994)_ (qv), with sexy \'Andie MacDowell\' (qv) in _Multiplicity (1996)_ (qv), twice in the same role, dogged Elmore Leonard character Agent Ray Nicolette, in _Jackie Brown (1997)_ (qv) and _Out of Sight (1998)_ (qv). He also played a killer in the mediocre thriller _Desperate Measures (1998)_ (qv).  In the 2000s, Keaton appeared in several productions with mixed success, including _Live from Baghdad (2002) (TV)_ (qv), _First Daughter (2004)_ (qv), and _Herbie Fully Loaded (2005)_ (qv). He also provided voices for characters in the animated films _Cars (2006)_ (qv), _Toy Story 3 (2010)_ (qv), and _Minions (2015)_ (qv).  He returned to major film roles in the 2010s, co-starring in _The Other Guys (2010)_ (qv), _RoboCop (2014)_ (qv) and _Need for Speed (2014)_ (qv). Also that year, Keaton starred alongside \'Zach Galifianakis\' (qv), \'Edward Norton (I)\' (qv), \'Emma Stone (III)\' (qv), and \'Naomi Watts\' (qv) in _Birdman or (The Unexpected Virtue of Ignorance) (2014)_ (qv), a film by _21 Grams (2003)_ (qv) and _Biutiful (2010)_ (qv) director \'Alejandro G. Iñárritu\' (qv). In the film, Keaton plays Riggan Thomson, a screen actor, famous for playing the iconic titular superhero, who puts on a Broadway play based on a Raymond Carver short story, to regain his former glory. Keaton\'s critically praised lead performance earned him a Golden Globe Award for Best Actor in a Musical or Comedy, the Critics\' Choice Award for Best Actor and Best Actor in a Comedy, and nominations for the Screen Actors Guild Award, British Academy Film Award, and Academy Award for Best Actor.  In 2015, he played a journalist in _Spotlight (2015/I)_ (qv), which, like Birdman, won the Academy Award for Best Picture. In 2016, he starred as \'Ray Kroc\' (qv), the developer of McDonald\'s, in the drama _The Founder (2016)_ (qv).  He is a visiting scholar at Carnegie Mellon University. \nfirehouse44 and Pedro Borges\n\n* \'Caroline McWilliams\' (qv) (5 June 1982 - 29 January 1990) (divorced); 1 child \n\n* Wild hyper-kinetic energy in comedies \n\n* When he realized he needed to change his name to join the union, he was in the K\'s for surnames and thought it inoffensive so chose Keaton. It is a misconception that it was after \'Diane Keaton\' (qv). \n* Was in a relationship with \'Courteney Cox\' (qv) from 1989-95. \n* Has a home in Pacific Palisades (CA) plus ranches in Santa Barbara (CA) and Montana. The 1000-acre Montana ranch, where he grows hay and raises cattle, features a four-bedroom cedar-and-stone ranch house. \n* \'Tim Burton (I)\' (qv) cast him in the title role of _Batman (1989)_ (qv) because he thought that Keaton was the only actor who could believably portray someone who has the kind of darkly obsessive personality that the character has. There was a great deal of fan anger over his selection, forcing the studio to release an advance trailer both to show that Keaton could do the role well and that the movie would not be a campy parody like the television series _\"Batman\" (1966)_ (qv). \n* Attended and graduated from Montour High School in Robinson Township, PA. \n* Is the youngest of seven siblings. Has three brothers and three sisters. \n* Has one son with ex-wife \'Caroline McWilliams\' (qv): \'Sean Douglas (VI)\' (qv) (born May 27, 1983). \n* Decided to change his name when he began acting because there was already a \'Michael Douglas (I)\' (qv) in movies and a \'Mike Douglas (I)\' (qv) in broadcasting. While he uses a stage name, he has never legally changed his name to Michael Keaton. \n* Was the first of only three actors to reprise the role of Batman in major, live-action films (_Batman (1989)_ (qv)/_Batman Returns (1992)_ (qv). \'Adam West (I)\' (qv) did only one movie (_Batman: The Movie (1966)_ (qv)) as Batman (along with the live-action TV series _\"Batman\" (1966)_ (qv) and voice-work) and \'Kevin Conroy (I)\' (qv) has only done voice-work as Batman. \'Christian Bale\' (qv) is the second actor to play the role more than once with (_Batman Begins (2005)_ (qv) followed by _The Dark Knight (2008)_ (qv) and for a third time in _The Dark Knight Rises (2012)_ (qv)). Most recently, \'Ben Affleck\' (qv) has appeared in _Batman v Superman: Dawn of Justice (2016)_ (qv), _Suicide Squad (2016)_ (qv) and _Justice League (2017)_ (qv). \n* Has played Agent Ray Nicolette in _Jackie Brown (1997)_ (qv) and again in _Out of Sight (1998)_ (qv). \n* Started his career as a stagehand in _\"MisteRogers\' Neighborhood\" (1968)_ (qv) (he operated \"Picture, Picture\"), and in 2004 he produced a documentary on Rogers, _Fred Rogers: America\'s Favorite Neighbor (2004) (TV)_ (qv). \n* Turned down the role of the ill-fated mad scientist Dr. Seth Brundle in \'David Cronenberg\' (qv)\'s remake _The Fly (1986)_ (qv). The role eventually went to \'Jeff Goldblum\' (qv). \n* Is a Second City alumnus - a member of the Los Angeles branch. \n* According to \'Mike Myers (I)\' (qv) on _\"Revealed with Jules Asner\" (2001)_ (qv), Keaton saw him perform at Second City Toronto. After the show ended, Keaton went to personally congratulate Myers and said, \"Keep up the great work.\" Myers would soon work with Keaton on an episode of _\"Saturday Night Live\" (1975)_ (qv) when Keaton was guest host. \n* His son, \'Sean Douglas (VI)\' (qv), plays keyboard for a band called \"The Hatch\". \n* Has appeared with the late \'Christopher Reeve\' (qv) in _Speechless (1994)_ (qv). Keaton and Reeve played DC Comics\' two most iconic characters, Batman and Superman. \n* He was originally to play the role of Dr. Jack Shephard on the television series _\"Lost\" (2004)_ (qv), with the understanding that the character would be killed off early on in the series. Keaton later had to walk away from the role when the creators decided not to kill off the doctor. \'Matthew Fox (I)\' (qv) ended up playing the character. \n* Was parodied by \'Matthew Perry (I)\' (qv) on _\"Saturday Night Live\" (1975)_ (qv). \n* Was offered the role of either Peter Venkman or Egon Spengler in _Ghostbusters (1984)_ (qv) but turned down both roles, which went to \'Bill Murray (I)\' (qv) and \'Harold Ramis\' (qv), respectively. \n* An avid fan of the Pittsburgh Steelers, he grew up about five miles from former Steelers coach \'Bill Cowher\' (qv)\'s hometown of Crafton, PA. \n* Enjoys snowboarding, golf, mountain biking, fly-fishing and riding horses on his California and Montana ranches. \n* One of his favorite hobbies is fly-fishing, a hobby he shares with his _Night Shift (1982)_ (qv) co-star \'Henry Winkler\' (qv). \n* Was 40 years old when filming _Batman Returns (1992)_ (qv), which made him the oldest actor at the time to play Batman in a live-action film. He was later succeeded by \'Ben Affleck\' (qv), who was 41 years old when cast in the role in _Batman v Superman: Dawn of Justice (2016)_ (qv). \n* Is the fourth--and shortest--actor to play Bruce Wayne/Batman. \n* His father had English, Scottish, Scots-Irish (Northern Irish), and German ancestry, while his mother was from an Irish family. \n* In an interview, he named _Mr. Mom (1983)_ (qv), _Gung Ho (1986)_ (qv), _Clean and Sober (1988)_ (qv), _Beetlejuice (1988)_ (qv), _Batman (1989)_ (qv), _Much Ado About Nothing (1993)_ (qv), _The Paper (1994)_ (qv) and _Live from Baghdad (2002) (TV)_ (qv) as favorite films of his own, with _Beetlejuice (1988)_ (qv) as his top pick. \n* Almost reprised his role as Batman/Bruce Wayne in \'Tim Burton (I)\' (qv)\'s unmade \"Superman Lives\", even though \'Val Kilmer\' (qv) had played the role in his place in _Batman Forever (1995)_ (qv). \n* When asked in an interview which historical figure he wished he could play, his choice was Hall of Fame baseball player \'Ted Williams (I)\' (qv). \n* Is an avid news junkie and at one point had considered a career in journalism. Has played a journalist in three films: _The Paper (1994)_ (qv), _Live from Baghdad (2002) (TV)_ (qv) and _Spotlight (2015/I)_ (qv). \n* Has worked with three generations of actresses: \'Melanie Griffith (I)\' (qv) and her mother \'Tippi Hedren\' (qv) in _Pacific Heights (1990)_ (qv), and Griffith\'s daughter \'Dakota Johnson\' (qv) in _Need for Speed (2014)_ (qv). \n* As of 2016 has appeared in three films that were nominated for the Best Picture Oscar: _Toy Story 3 (2010)_ (qv), _Birdman or (The Unexpected Virtue of Ignorance) (2014)_ (qv) and _Spotlight (2015/I)_ (qv). Of those, Birdman and Spotlight are winners in the category. \n* He was directed by \'Ron Howard (I)\' (qv) in three films: _Night Shift (1982)_ (qv), _Gung Ho (1986)_ (qv) and _The Paper (1994)_ (qv). Same goes with \'Tim Burton (I)\' (qv): _Beetlejuice (1988)_ (qv), _Batman (1989)_ (qv) and _Batman Returns (1992)_ (qv). \n* Has appeared with \'Geena Davis\' (qv) in _Beetlejuice (1988)_ (qv) and _Speechless (1994)_ (qv), and had he accepted the lead role in _The Fly (1986)_ (qv), this would be their third film (and the first they would be making together). \n* As of 2017 he remains as the only living Bruce Wayne/Batman actor to not be directed by \'Terrence Malick\' (qv). \'George Clooney\' (qv) appeared in _The Thin Red Line (1998)_ (qv); \'Ben Affleck\' (qv) appeared in _To the Wonder (2012)_ (qv); \'Christian Bale\' (qv) appeared in _The New World (2005)_ (qv) and _Knight of Cups (2015)_ (qv); and \'Val Kilmer\' (qv) is due to appear in Malick\'s upcoming film, which also stars Bale. \'Adam West (I)\' (qv), who played Bruce Wayne/Batman in \'Batman: The Movie\' (1966) and the 60\'s TV series, died 9 June 2017 without ever being directed by \'Terrence Malick\' (qv). \n* He was awarded a Star on the Hollywood Walk of Fame at 6931 Hollywood Blvd. on July 28, 2016. \n* He was awarded Officer of the Order of Arts and Letters by French culture minister \'Fleur Pellerin\' (qv) on January 18, 2016. \n* A Democrat, he endorsed President \'Barack Obama\' (qv) for re-election in 2012 and Sen. \'Bernie Sanders\' (qv) for the Democratic nomination in 2016. \n* He was considered for the role of Harry Belafonte in _When Harry Met Sally... (1989)_ (qv), which went to \'Billy Crystal\' (qv). \n* He was considered for the role of Andrew Beckett in _Philadelphia (1993)_ (qv), which went to \'Tom Hanks\' (qv). \n* He turned down the lead role in _Clean Slate (1994)_ (qv) in favor of _My Life (1993)_ (qv). \n* \'John McTiernan\' (qv) wanted him to star in _The 13th Warrior (1999)_ (qv), but the studio did not want him. \n* He was considered for the role of Jack Traven in _Speed (1994)_ (qv), which went to \'Keanu Reeves\' (qv). \n* He turned down \'John C. Reilly (I)\' (qv)\'s role in _Kong: Skull Island (2017)_ (qv) due to scheduling conflicts. \n* He was considered for the lead role in _Police Academy (1984)_ (qv), which went to \'Steve Guttenberg (I)\' (qv). \n* He was offered the role of Chili Palmer in _Get Shorty (1995)_ (qv), which went to \'John Travolta (I)\' (qv). He would later play Ray Nicolette in _Jackie Brown (1997)_ (qv) and _Out of Sight (1998)_ (qv), which were also based on \'Elmore Leonard\' (qv) books. \n* He turned down the role of Alan Bauer in _Splash (1984)_ (qv), which went to \'Tom Hanks\' (qv). \n* He was considered for one of \'Jack Nicholson (I)\' (qv)\'s roles in _Mars Attacks! (1996)_ (qv). \n* He was considered for the role of Jim Garrison in _JFK (1991)_ (qv), which went to \'Kevin Costner\' (qv). \n* He was considered for the role of Roy Munson in _Kingpin (1996)_ (qv), which went to \'Woody Harrelson\' (qv). \n* He was offered the male lead in _Cutthroat Island (1995)_ (qv), which went to \'Matthew Modine\' (qv). \n* He worked on a Navajo reservation in New Mexico when he was age 21. \n* Is a huge fan of \'Katy Perry\' (qv). \n* Has worked with all three of \'Tom Cruise\' (qv)\'s ex-wives: \'Mimi Rogers (I)\' (qv) in _Gung Ho (1986)_ (qv) as boyfriend and girlfriend; \'Nicole Kidman\' (qv) in _My Life (1993)_ (qv) as husband and wife; and \'Katie Holmes (I)\' (qv) in _First Daughter (2004)_ (qv) as father and daughter. \n* Not only did he play Bruce Wayne/Batman in \'Tim Burton (I)\' (qv)\'s Batman films, he has acted alongside all three actors to play mentor figures in \'Christopher Nolan (I)\' (qv)\'s Dark Knight trilogy: \'Michael Caine (I)\' (qv) (Alfred) in _Quicksand (2003)_ (qv); \'Gary Oldman (I)\' (qv) (James Gordon) in _RoboCop (2014)_ (qv); and \'Morgan Freeman (I)\' (qv) (Lucius Fox) in _Clean and Sober (1988)_ (qv). \n* He was considered for Dr. Ian Malcolm in _Jurassic Park (1993)_ (qv). \n* He was considered for Josh Baskin in _Big (1988)_ (qv). \n* He was considered for King Koopa in _Super Mario Bros. (1993)_ (qv). \n* In the 1980s, Keaton bought a ranch near Big Timber, Montana, where he spends much of his time. \n* Was considered for the role of Lt. Col. Kazinski in _Jarhead (2005)_ (qv). \n* During his appearance on the WTF Podcast with Marc Maron, he stated that he has never watched the completed version of _Batman Returns (1992)_ (qv). He went on to explain that he only took the role because he needed money for a real-estate deal. \n* He was considered for Connor MacLeod in _Highlander (1986)_ (qv). \n* Was considered for the role of Jack Crawford in _The Silence of the Lambs (1991)_ (qv). \n* He was considered for Alan Parrish in _Jumanji (1995)_ (qv). \n* He was considered for Phil Connors in _Groundhog Day (1993)_ (qv), but was deemed \"too nice\" for the role. \n* He was considered for \'Richard Gere\' (qv)\'s role in _Internal Affairs (1990)_ (qv). \n* He was considered for Martin Riggs in _Lethal Weapon (1987)_ (qv). \n* He was considered for many roles in _Pulp Fiction (1994)_ (qv) - Vincent Vega (which went to \'John Travolta (I)\' (qv)), Mr. Wolf (which went to \'Harvey Keitel\' (qv)) and Lance (which went to \'Eric Stoltz\' (qv). \n* He was considered for Willy Wonka in _Charlie and the Chocolate Factory (2005)_ (qv). \n* He was originally cast in the role of Sean Devine in _Mystic River (2003)_ (qv) and filmed some scenes but he and director \'Clint Eastwood\' (qv) had creative differences on the project and Keaton opted to leave the film. \n* Was considered for the role of Dr. Curtis McCabe in _Vanilla Sky (2001)_ (qv). Was considered for the role of Captain Jack Sparrow in _Pirates of the Caribbean: The Curse of the Black Pearl (2003)_ (qv). \n* He was considered for Axel Foley in _Beverly Hills Cop (1984)_ (qv). \n* Keaton is a Democrat. He endorsed President \'Barack Obama\' (qv) for re-election in 2012 and Senator \'Bernie Sanders\' (qv) for the Democratic nomination in 2016. \n* Was originally slated to star in _The Purple Rose of Cairo (1985)_ (qv) and actually did film some scenes, but \'Woody Allen\' (qv) decided this was not working and replaced him with \'Jeff Daniels (I)\' (qv). \n\n* One of Michael his early jobs in \"Hollywood\" was pulling \'Mr. Trolley\' for \"Mr. Rogers Neighborhood\". \n* Voice of George Washington in commercials advertising the new dollar coin. \n* TV commercial for The History Channel (2001) \n* Mr. Mom; co-star Terri Garr; Martin Mull \n\n* [after interviewer \'Michael Parkinson (II)\' (qv) commented on his birth name being Michael Douglas] Yeah, I had to change my name because there were two other actors registered at Equity with that name. One of them is doing quite well from what I understand, the other is making cheap porn movies--like _Basic Instinct (1992)_ (qv). \n* [comparing making _Batman Returns (1992)_ (qv) to the first _Batman (1989)_ (qv) film] In some ways this one was harder, because I felt like I was doing an impersonation of myself. Which, aside from being nearly impossible, is really weird. \n* [on his decision not to reprise his role as Batman in _Batman Forever (1995)_ (qv)] I was waiting in line for another movie and just kind of poked my head in . . . watched about 10 minutes. I saw enough to know that I made the right decision. \n* [When asked what he thought of _Batman Begins (2005)_ (qv) before its release] My prediction, I don\'t know anything about it, but I feel this way about it. It\'s gonna be good, because he\'s a really good actor [\'Christian Bale\' (qv)] and that\'s a really good director [\'Christopher Nolan (I)\' (qv)]. And they\'ve had years and years and years, and hundreds of millions of dollars, or at least tens of millions of dollars to figure it out. I say it\'s gonna be good. I picture it\'s gonna be good. And also, I swear to God it\'s not an \"I told you so\", it\'s maybe an interesting thing, that when I didn\'t like the third script . . . I just said \"I really don\'t like this, and I don\'t want to do it\", \'cause what I wanted to do, is what I\'m told and I don\'t know if this is true yet so don\'t hold me to this until I see it, but I\'m told it\'s more a prequel. And that was what I thought would\'ve been a hip way to go the third time. This guy is so endlessly fascinating potentially, why not go and see how he got there. \n* [when asked if he was ever offered a villain role in a superhero film] No, but it would be fun. I don\'t think I\'d take Jack\'s [\'Jack Nicholson (I)\' (qv)] stance on it. I think it\'d be fun because those are the roles where you get to chew it up. I\'ll always stand by the first \"Batman\". Even for its imperfections, people will never know how hard that movie was to do. A lot of that still holds up. \n* [on filming _Batman (1989)_ (qv) in London] It was a lonely time for me, which was great for the character, I suppose. I would run at night in London just trying to get tired enough so I could sleep. I didn\'t talk to people much. My little boy was a toddler, and the woman I was married to at the time, we were not together but we were trying to figure it out and get back together. It was me in London, alone, and my sleep during that whole movie was never right. As often as I could, I was getting on the Concorde and trying to get back to spend some time with my kid . . . It was an extremely difficult undertaking and [\'Tim Burton (I)\' (qv)] os a shy guy, especially back then, and there was so much pressure. We were in England for a long time shooting at Pinewood and it was long, difficult nights in that dank, dark, cold place, and we never knew if it was really working. There was no guarantee that any of this was going to play correctly when it was all said and done. There had never been a movie like it before. There was a lot of risk, too, with Jack [\'Jack Nicholson (I)\' (qv)] looking the way he did and me stepping out in this new way. The pressure was on everybody. You could feel it. \n* [2011, on his work ethic] I played a lot of sports when I was a kid so I get in that ballgame mindset of being really, really respectful, but at same time saying to yourself, \"Don\'t back down a single inch, hang with these guys if you can.\" If they throw it high and tight you have to stand in there, you can\'t take yourself out of that moment. \n* [2011, on _Night Shift (1982)_ (qv)] The character I invented was a combination of some people I knew and some things I made up, and afterward there [were other projects and offers] that would have meant trying to repeat that over and over, to be the \"glib young man\", whatever that is, but that held no interest for me. I literally thought the idea of all this, when you do it for a living, is to play a lot of different things. If you do the same thing over and over, that will eventually start to close in on you. \n* [2011, on _Beetlejuice (1988)_ (qv)] From an art perspective, I don\'t know how you get better than \"Beetlejuice\". In terms of originality and a look, it\'s 100% unique. If you consider the process of taking something from someone\'s mind--meaning \'Tim Burton (I)\' (qv)]--and putting it on the screen, I think that movie is incomparable. \n* [2011, on playing Beetlejuice] I wanted him to be pure electricity, that\'s why the hair just sticks out. At my house, I started creating a walk and a voice. I got some teeth. I wanted to be scary in the look and then use the voice to add a dash of goofiness that, in a way, would make it even scarier. I wanted something kind of moldy to it, too. [\'Tim Burton (I)\' (qv)] had the striped-suit idea and we added the big eyes. I think that movie will go forever because it\'s 100% original. \n* [2011, on filming _Batman Returns (1992)_ (qv)] We got to be back home [filming in Burbank] so that made me happy. It was quite the cast with \'Michelle Pfeiffer\' (qv) and \'Danny DeVito\' (qv) and everyone. It wasn\'t as satisfying to me when I saw it, but maybe that\'s because the bar was set so high on the first one. I think I only watched it one time. I knew we were in trouble in talks for the third one when certain people started the conversation with \"Why does it have to be so dark?\" \"Why does he have to be so depressed?\" \"Shouldn\'t there be more color in this thing?\" I knew I was headed for trouble and that it wasn\'t a road I was going to go down. \n* [2011, on _Clean and Sober (1988)_ (qv)] The subject matter was so difficult, but oddly everyone really had fun on the shoot. One great thing about being an actor, too, is that if you have a pulse you learn something. That\'s one of the great joys and bonuses of it. You\'re forced to ask certain questions. \n* [2011, on _Much Ado About Nothing (1993)_ (qv)] That\'s a movie where I said, \"I can\'t do this\" and it ended up being probably one of my top five experiences ever. I had to find a way in; I didn\'t really know what to do, quite frankly . . . In the end, [\'Kenneth Branagh\' (qv)] didn\'t get scared off by my unorthodox approach, he embraced it and was really hands-on, thankfully. It was literally like acting in another language. I had taken maybe one two-day Shakespearean class in my life, so I had no knowledge. \n* [2011, on filming _The Paper (1994)_ (qv)] It\'s an awful lot of fun to be in an ensemble, especially when you\'re talking about \'Glenn Close\' (qv), \'Robert Duvall\' (qv) and that level of actor. It was also the first time I met Duvall. People were nervous on the set when he was coming in; he\'s a presence, somebody to [reckon] with. I just loved it. I had a ball being there with him. It felt like the first time I acted with \'Jack Nicholson (I)\' (qv). These guys are in their very nature larger-than-life personalities, and then they\'re great actors on top of that and then they\'re iconic on top of that. \n* [2011, on his life as an actor] I never saw what I did for a living as who I am. But if there\'s a job in the world where that can get blurry, this is the one. The line gets really blurry for a lot of people, and for understandable reasons just as you go through life and this business. You don\'t have to be especially weak to become extremely self-involved in this business, and I just never wanted to go down that road . . . \'Alan Arkin\' (qv) said to me once that he wanted to have a really big life and a really good career. And I think that\'s really sane. \n* [on the backlash over his casting in _Batman (1989)_ (qv)] When they hung me in effigy, that was, for me, harsh. \n* [on \'Michelle Pfeiffer\' (qv)] What impressed me about Michelle is that she\'s a California beach chick, no elevated education, but when you\'re smart you just get smarter. \n* [on being asked if he got jealous when other actors played Batman] No. Do you know why? Because I\'m Batman. I\'m very secure in that. \n* [Paying tribute to \'Michael Gough (I)\' (qv)] To Mick--my butler, my confidant, my friend, my Alfred. I love you. God bless. \n* I never really thought about being famous. I always wanted to be good. That\'s all I really ever wanted to be, was good at what I did. When I go to work, I want to see how good I can get. And that\'s the great thing about my job - it\'s a never-ending quest. \n\n* _Game 6 (2005)_ (qv) -> $100 per day \n* _Batman (1989)_ (qv) -> $5,000,000 \n\n* \"Parade Magazine\" (USA), 15 January 2017, pg. 10-11, by: Dotson Rader, \"A Star Is Reborn\" \n* \"Maxim\" (USA), December 2015, Iss. 209, pg. 56, 58, by: Eric Spitznagel, \"Go West\" \n* \"Episodi\" (Finland), June 2005, Iss. 5, pg. 58-61, by: Tuukka Jantti, \"Haastattelussa Michael Keaton\" \n* \"Cinema\" (Hungary), December 1999, Iss. 97, pg. 98, by: Ralf Blau, \"Az ember, aki fél a kék élelmiszerektõl\" \n* \"Vox\" (Hungary), May 1998, Iss. 13, pg. 35, \"Michael Keaton\" \n* \"Starlog\" (USA), November 1996, Iss. 232, by: Dan Yakir, \"Recurring Character\" \n* \"Premiere\" (USA), July 1996, Vol. 9, Iss. 11, pg. 84-88+102, by: Corie Brown \n* \"Première\" (France), 1996, Iss. 234, pg. 39, by: Louis Dreyfus \n* \"Diário de Notícias\" (Portugal), 1996, Iss. 46609, pg. 39, by: Rui Henriques Coimbra \n* \"Empire\" (UK), 1996, Iss. 88, pg. 74-76+78+80, by: David Eimer, \"Beside Himself\" \n* \"Empire\" (UK), 1994, Iss. 60, pg. 44-45, by: Jenny Cooney \n* \"Playboy\" (USA), July 1992, Vol. 39, Iss. 7, pg. 49-60, by: Lawrence Linderman, \"Playboy Interview\" \n\n* \"New York Post\" (USA), 20 January 2017, Vol. 216, Iss. 66, pg. 35, by: Kyle Smith, \"LOVIN\' IT! - Michael Keaton cooks up movie magic as McDonald\'s sleazy savior in \'The Founder\'\" \n* \"USA Today\" (USA), 17 January 2017, Vol. 35, Iss. 86, pg. 2D, by: Brian Truitt, \"\'Founder\' is a sweet \'n\' salty biopic: Keaton sinks his teeth into story of McDonald\'s Kroc\" \n* \"USA Today\" (USA), 16 January 2017, Vol. 35, Iss. 85, pg. 1D-2D, by: Bryan Alexander, \"KEATON\'S ON A ROLL WITH \'THE FOUNDER\' - He spreads his wings again after \'Batman\' and \'Birdman\' as a ravenous fast-food icon\" \n* \"Los Angeles Times\" (USA), 9 December 2016, Vol. 136, Iss. 6, pg. E1, E18, by: Glenn Whipp, \"Opening flips like a burger: False starts bedevil the fast-food story \'Founder\'s\' release date. But Weinstein assures t\'s behind the film\" \n* \"USA Today\" (USA), 1 March 2016, Vol. 34, Iss. 119, pg. 3B, by: Rem Rieder, \"OSCAR WIN FOR \'SPOTLIGHT\' GOOD NEWS FOR JOURNALISM\" \n* \"Parade\" (USA), 16 November 2014, pg. 2, by: n/a, \"The Keaton File\" \n* \"The New York Times\" (USA), 20 January 2008, Vol. 157, Iss. 54,195, pg. AR11-AR12, by: David M. Halbfinger, \"Michael Keaton Never Expected To Be the Director\" \n* \"Cowboys & Indians\" (USA), October 2002, Vol. 10, Iss. 6, pg. 112-120, by: Eric O\'Keefe, \"The story of one star\'s search for the ideal antidote to Hollywood\" \n* \"TV Filmes\" (Portugal), 1997, Iss. 12, pg. 49, by: José Vaz Pereira \n\n* \"Playboy\" (USA), May 1995, Vol. 42, Iss. 5, pg. 159, by: Armando Gallo, \"Grapevine: Big Al, Miked\" \n* \"Playboy\" (USA), November 1992, Vol. 39, Iss. 11, pg. 142+143, by: Bruce Williamson, \"Sex In Cinema 1992\" \n* \"Playboy\" (USA), December 1989, Vol. 36, Iss. 12, pg. 176, by: Jim Harwood, \"Holy Sex Stars of 1989!\" \n* \"Playboy\" (USA), November 1988, Vol. 35, Iss. 11, pg. 139, by: Bruce Williamson, \"Sex in Cinema 1988\" \n* \"Playboy\" (USA), January 1987, Vol. 34, Iss. 1, pg. 119, by: Hollis Wayne, \"The New Man\" \n\n* \"Parade Magazine\" (USA), 15 January 2017 \n* \"Cowboys & Indians\" (USA), October 2002, Vol. 10, Iss. 6 \n* \"Prevue\" (USA), September 1992, Vol. 2, Iss. 48 \n* \"Starlog\" (USA), August 1992, Iss. 181 \n* \"Premiere\" (USA), July 1992, Iss. 7 \n* \"American Cinematographer\" (USA), July 1992, Vol. 73, Iss. 7 \n* \"Starlog\" (USA), May 1992, Iss. 178 \n* \"Starlog Yearbook\" (USA), 1990, Vol. 6 \n* \"Starlog\" (USA), October 1989, Iss. 147 \n* \"Starlog\" (USA), August 1989, Iss. 145 \n* \"Empire\" (UK), August 1989, Iss. 2 \n* \"Starlog\" (USA), July 1989, Iss. 144 \n* \"Rolling Stone\" (USA), 29 June 1989 \n* \"Starlog\" (USA), June 1989, Iss. 143 \n* \"Starlog\" (USA), May 1989, Iss. 142 \n* \"Starlog Yearbook\" (USA), 1989, Vol. 5 \n* \"Starlog\" (USA), May 1988, Vol. 11, Iss. 130'),
(366770,'Kidman, Nicole','Nicole Mary Kidman \nNic \n20 June 1967, Honolulu, Hawaii, USA \n5\' 11\" \nElegant blonde Nicole Kidman, known as one of Hollywood\'s top Australian imports, was actually born in Honolulu, Hawaii, while her Australian parents were there on educational visas.  Kidman is the daughter of Janelle Ann (Glenny), a nursing instructor, and Antony David Kidman, a biochemist and clinical psychologist. She is of English, Irish, and Scottish descent. Shortly after her birth, the family moved to Washington, D.C., where Nicole\'s father pursued his research on breast cancer, and then, three years later, made the pilgrimage back to her parents\' native Sydney in Australia, where Nicole was raised. Young Nicole\'s first love was ballet, but she eventually took up mime and drama as well (her first stage role was a bleating sheep in an elementary school Christmas pageant). In her adolescent years, acting edged out the other arts and became a kind of refuge -- as her classmates sought out fun in the sun, the fair-skinned Kidman retreated to dark rehearsal halls to practice her craft. She worked regularly at the Philip Street Theater, where she once received a personal letter of praise and encouragement from audience member \'Jane Campion\' (qv) (then a film student). Kidman eventually dropped out of high school to pursue acting full-time. She broke into movies at age 16, landing a role in the Australian holiday favorite _Bush Christmas (1983)_ (qv). That appearance touched off a flurry of film and television offers, including a lead in _BMX Bandits (1983)_ (qv) and a turn as a schoolgirl-turned-protester in the miniseries _\"Vietnam\" (1987)_ (qv) (for which she won her first Australian Film Institute Award). With the help of an American agent, she eventually made her US debut opposite \'Sam Neill\' (qv) in the at-sea thriller _Dead Calm (1989)_ (qv).  Kidman\'s next casting coup scored her more than exposure. While starring as \'Tom Cruise\' (qv)\'s doctor/love interest in the racetrack romance _Days of Thunder (1990)_ (qv), she won over the Hollywood hunk hook, line and sinker. After a whirlwind courtship (and decent box office returns), the couple wed on December 24, 1990. Determined not to let her new marital status overshadow her fledgling career, the actress pressed on. She appeared as a catty high school senior in the Australian film _Flirting (1991)_ (qv), then as \'Dustin Hoffman\' (qv)\'s moll in the gangster flick _Billy Bathgate (1991)_ (qv). She reunited with Cruise for _Far and Away (1992)_ (qv), the story of young Irish lovers who flee to America in the late 1800s, and starred opposite \'Michael Keaton\' (qv) in the tear-tugger _My Life (1993)_ (qv). Despite her steady employment, critics and moviegoers still had not quite warmed to Kidman as a leading lady. She tried to spice up her image by seducing \'Val Kilmer\' (qv) in _Batman Forever (1995)_ (qv), but achieved her real breakthrough with \'Gus Van Sant\' (qv)\'s _To Die For (1995)_ (qv). As a fame-crazed housewife determined to eliminate any obstacle in her path, Kidman proved that she had an impressive range and deadly comic timing. She took home a Golden Globe and several critics\' awards for the performance. In 1996, Kidman stepped into a corset to work with her countrywoman and onetime admirer, \'Jane Campion\' (qv), on the adaptation of \'Henry James (I)\' (qv)\'s _The Portrait of a Lady (1996)_ (qv). A few months later, she tore across the screen as a nuclear weapons expert in _The Peacemaker (1997)_ (qv), adding \"action star\" to her professional repertoire.  She and Cruise then disappeared into a notoriously long, secretive shoot for \'Stanley Kubrick\' (qv)\'s sexual thriller _Eyes Wide Shut (1999)_ (qv). The couple\'s on-screen shenanigans prompted an increase in public speculation about their sex life (rumors had long been circulating that their marriage was a cover-up for Cruise\'s homosexuality); tired of denying tabloid attacks, they successfully sued The Star for a story alleging that they needed a sex therapist to coach them through love scenes. Family life has always been a priority for Kidman. Born to social activists (mother was a feminist; father, a labor advocate), Nicole and her little sister, \'Antonia Kidman\' (qv), discussed current events around the dinner table and participated in their parents\' campaigns by passing out pamphlets on street corners. When her mother was diagnosed with breast cancer, 17-year-old Nicole stopped working and took a massage course so that she could provide physical therapy (her mother eventually beat the cancer). She and Cruise adopted two children: Isabella Jane (born 1993) and Connor Antony (born 1995). Despite their rock-solid image, the couple announced in early 2001 that they were separating due to career conflicts. Her marriage to Cruise ended mid-summer of 2001. \nIMDb Editors\n\n* \'Tom Cruise\' (qv) (24 December 1990 - 8 August 2001) (divorced); 2 children \n* \'Keith Urban (II)\' (qv) (25 June 2006 - present); 2 children \n\n* Blonde hair and blue eyes \n* Statuesque, model-like figure \n* Her independent films often feature the theme of grief and sorrow, such as _Birth (2004)_ (qv) and _Rabbit Hole (2010)_ (qv) \n* Often portrays upper-class women in epics, such as Ada Monroe in _Cold Mountain (2003)_ (qv), Marisa Coulter in _The Golden Compass (2007)_ (qv), Lady Sarah Ashley in _Australia (2008)_ (qv) and Gertrude Bell in _Queen of the Desert (2015)_ (qv). \n\n* 2000: She and her husband \'Tom Cruise\' (qv) donated to \'Hillary Clinton\' (qv)\'s campaign for a seat in the U.S. Senate representing New York state. \n* Suffered a broken rib while rehearsing a dance routine for the movie _Moulin Rouge! (2001)_ (qv). Production was halted while she recovered. \n* Chosen by People Magazine as one of the 50 Most Beautiful People in the World in 1996, 1999 and 2002. In 2004, she was one of People Magazine\'s \"50 Most Beautiful People for Most Wanted Skin\". She ranked as #69 in FHM\'s \"100 Sexiest Women in the World 2005\" special supplement and in August 2008 she was voted the fifth Sexiest Female Movie Star in the Australian Empire Magazine. \n* Wore a corset while filming _The Portrait of a Lady (1996)_ (qv) to take her waist down to 19\". \n* Spent two weeks in bed after filming of _The Portrait of a Lady (1996)_ (qv) - diagnosed as suffering from \"emotional stress\". \n* 10/99: Crusader against child abuse. \"Children should be allowed to grow up without fear of cruelty.\". \n* Listed in \"People Weekly\"s \"Most Intriguing People\" list of 1995. In 2001, she was awarded \"Celebrity of the Year\" by E!, \"Entertainer of the Year\" by Entertainment Weekly and named one of People Magazine\'s \"25 Most Intriguing People of 2001\". Also, Premiere Magazine ranked her as #48 on a list of the \"Greatest Movie Stars of All Time\" in their 2005 feature \"Stars in Our Constellation\". Finally, she ranked #31 in Premiere\'s annual Power 100 List. Had ranked #83 in 2002. \n* Played a young girl named Grace on a sketch on _\"Saturday Night Live\" (1975)_ (qv) with \'Mike Myers (I)\' (qv), the next time she played a character named Grace was her movie _The Others (2001)_ (qv). She played a character named Grace for the third time in _Dogville (2003)_ (qv) and for the fourth time as the protagonist in _Grace of Monaco (2014)_ (qv). \n* She was supposed to be the star of _Panic Room (2002)_ (qv), but she hurt herself in a stunt for _Moulin Rouge! (2001)_ (qv). So, she played the voice of \'Jodie Foster\' (qv)\'s divorced husband\'s wife. \n* Older sister of Australian television personality \'Antonia Kidman\' (qv). \n* Although naturally left-handed, she taught herself to write right-handed for her role in _The Hours (2002)_ (qv), where she played the right-handed author \'Virginia Woolf (I)\' (qv). \n* Received a star on the Hollywood Walk of Fame at 6801 Hollywood Boulevard in Hollywood, California on January 13, 2003. \n* Found out about her first Academy Award nomination, for _Moulin Rouge! (2001)_ (qv), while shooting _Dogville (2003)_ (qv) in Sweden. She was in the middle of shooting a scene when someone held up a sign with the announcement. \n* First Australian actress to win the Best Actress Academy Award. \n* Prior to the release of _Australia (2008)_ (qv), she was elected alongside fellow Aussie \'Hugh Jackman\' (qv) as one of the favorite upcoming romantic couples on screen. \n* Originally cast as Mrs. Smith in _Mr. & Mrs. Smith (2005)_ (qv). She was the one who suggested \'Brad Pitt\' (qv) as the co-lead. \n* Has appeared in _My Life (1993)_ (qv) with \'Michael Keaton\' (qv), _Batman Forever (1995)_ (qv) with \'Val Kilmer\' (qv), _The Peacemaker (1997)_ (qv) with \'George Clooney\' (qv) and _The Portrait of a Lady (1996)_ (qv) with \'Christian Bale\' (qv). All four actors have played Batman in a movie. She also appeared in _To Die For (1995)_ (qv) with \'Casey Affleck\' (qv), whose brother \'Ben Affleck\' (qv) has also played Batman. \n* She was awarded the Special Award at the London Evening Standard Theatre Awards for her special and significant contributions to London Theatre for her performance in \"The Blue Room\". She was also nominated for a \'Laurence Olivier\' (qv) Theatre Award in 1999 (1998 season) for Best Actress for her performance in \"The Blue Room\". \n* A very tall woman at nearly 5\' 11\", she actually stood about 4 inches taller than ex-husband \'Tom Cruise\' (qv), and rarely wore heels when seen publicly with him so that they could appear similar in height. Since their divorce, she is rarely seen on the red carpet without high heels, and she often meets 6\' 4\" actors in the eye. She was already 5\' 9\" when she was age 13. \n* Scored an IQ of 132+ \n* While she usually commands $10 million+ for a movie, she was willing to receive only $500,000 for her role in \"Eucalyptus\" (2006), that eventually was scrapped. Produced by \"Fox Searchlight\", its financing guideline showed that each actor signed on to the project could receive no more than $500,000, in order to keep costs low. This movie was set to be produced in Australia, another reason she decided to accept it. She was slated to star alongside \'Russell Crowe\' (qv) in the movie, but the project was ultimately dropped. \n* Has never used a body double for her nude scenes. \n* Replaced \'Rene Russo (I)\' (qv) as Dr. Chase Meridian in _Batman Forever (1995)_ (qv) when \'Val Kilmer\' (qv) replaced \'Michael Keaton\' (qv). \n* Likes the poetry of \'Anne Carson (V)\' (qv) and the writing of \'Philip Larkin (IV)\' (qv). \n* Writes short stories in her free time and hopes to publish them one day. She revealed in June 2014 that she\'s also working on her first screenplay for a feature film. \n* Is in the 50th-anniversary edition of the Guinness Book Of Records as the highest-paid actress in a commercial. She netted $3.71 million for her part in a four-minute \'Baz Luhrmann\' (qv)-directed Chanel No. 5 movie ad. The ad, costing $11 million a minute, is a short film titled \"No 5: The Film\". Guinness book editors noted the actress earned $928,800 per minute. Costumes designed by \'Karl Lagerfeld\' (qv) and score by Debussy. \n* Was considered for the role of Catwoman in _Batman Returns (1992)_ (qv), for the \'Meg Ryan (I)\' (qv) role in _Sleepless in Seattle (1993)_ (qv) and for Jenny Curran in _Forrest Gump (1994)_ (qv), which went to \'Robin Wright (V)\' (qv). She also auditioned for a part in _The English Patient (1996)_ (qv) but was turned down. \n* Had played a witch twice: once in _Practical Magic (1998)_ (qv) and again in _Bewitched (2005)_ (qv). Witches also featured in her film _The Golden Compass (2007)_ (qv). \n* Is a pianist, and did her own piano-playing in _Cold Mountain (2003)_ (qv). \n* Is highly conscious of avoiding excessive sun exposure, and is rarely seen during her downtime without a hat, sunglasses and wearing sunscreen. In fact, best friend \'Naomi Watts\' (qv) has said that \"Nicole won\'t cross the street without a hat.\" \n* In addition to having worked with all four of the most recent Batmans, she has also worked with both of the most recent actors to play Alfred Pennyworth. In _Batman Forever (1995)_ (qv), she appears with \'Michael Gough (I)\' (qv). In _Bewitched (2005)_ (qv), her father is played by \'Michael Caine (I)\' (qv). \n* Has one niece: Lucia (b. 1999) and two nephews: Hamish (b. 2001), James (b. 2003). \n* August 2004: Australian business magazine Business Review Weekly listed Kidman as the richest Australian woman under age 40, estimated at a worth of $155 million (Australian). \n* Is childhood friends with \'Peter Overton (I)\' (qv), a reporter for the Australian _\"60 Minutes\" (1979)_ (qv) program. Both lived in the same suburb of Sydney when they were children. \n* She has English, Irish and Scottish ancestry. \n* She and ex-husband \'Tom Cruise\' (qv) made three movies together: _Days of Thunder (1990)_ (qv), _Eyes Wide Shut (1999)_ (qv) and _Far and Away (1992)_ (qv). They met on the set of _Days of Thunder (1990)_ (qv). \n* Attended the same high school attended by \'Catherine Martin (I)\' (qv) (two time Oscar winner for costume design and direction on _Moulin Rouge! (2001)_ (qv) and wife of \'Baz Luhrmann\' (qv)) and legendary Australian actress \'Ruth Cracknell\' (qv). \n* Godmother of Harry (b. 19 September 2001), the son of \'Simon Baker (I)\' (qv) and \'Rebecca Rigg\' (qv). Riggs and Kidman are close friends. \n* Named goodwill ambassador for the United Nations Development Fund for Women. \n* Was listed as a potential nominee on both the 2006 and 2008 Razzie Award nominating ballots. She was suggested in the Worst Actress category on the 2006 ballot for her performance in _Bewitched (2005)_ (qv). And she was suggested again two years later on the 2007 ballot in the Worst Actress category again for her performance in _The Invasion (2007/I)_ (qv). She failed to receive either nominations. \n* 2006: Her performance as Suzanne Stone Maretto in _To Die For (1995)_ (qv) is ranked #40 on Premiere Magazine\'s 100 Greatest Performances of All Time. \n* 2006: Accompanied her boyfriend \'Keith Urban (II)\' (qv) to the Grammy Awards. It was their first public appearance together. \n* Is allergic to strawberries and scared of butterflies. \n* Wore a wedding gown at her wedding to \'Keith Urban (II)\' (qv) by the label Balenciaga. \n* Married \'Keith Urban (II)\' (qv) in the St. Patrick\'s church in Manly, a suburb of Sydney. The wedding ceremony lasted 35 minutes. 230 guests attended her wedding to \'Keith Urban (II)\' (qv). Among them were her best friend \'Naomi Watts\' (qv), \'Russell Crowe\' (qv), \'Baz Luhrmann\' (qv), \'Hugh Jackman\' (qv) and Nicole\'s two children Isabella and Conor. \'Jim Carrey\' (qv) was invited and greatly wanted to come, in fact he booked plane tickets to Australia, but last minute scheduling conflicts stopped him from going. \n* She asked her wedding guests not to buy any presents for her and \'Keith Urban (II)\' (qv) but to donate the money instead to some humanitarian organization. \n* She and \'Keith Urban (II)\' (qv) honeymooned in Tahiti -- more precisely the St. Regis in Bora Bora. \'Eva Longoria\' (qv) and her boyfriend Tony Parker were also coincidentally staying there at the time. \n* \'Baz Luhrmann\' (qv) read a love Psalm by New Zealand writer Joy Cowley (a nod to Urban\'s birth place) at her wedding to \'Keith Urban (II)\' (qv). \n* Three star performers sang at her wedding reception to \'Keith Urban (II)\' (qv). \'Hugh Jackman\' (qv) sang \"Tenterfield Saddler\", a favorite of Urban\'s. \'Keith Urban (II)\' (qv) sang his hit song \"Making Memories of Us\" to her. \'Neil Finn\' (qv) sang \"Fall At Your Feet\". \n* Dropped out of the role of \'Katharine Hepburn\' (qv) in _The Aviator (2004)_ (qv) due to scheduling conflicts. \n* Author \'Philip Pullman\' (qv) requested her specifically to play the role of Marisa Coulter in _The Golden Compass (2007)_ (qv), the movie adaptation of his book \"His Dark Materials: Northern Lights\" from 1995. Kidman was originally unsure if she would take the part when some producers from New Line Cinema offered it to her. However, with a beautiful letter, Pullman fully convinced her to take the role. Pullman knew she was right for the part after he saw her performance in _To Die For (1995)_ (qv) and with a producer from New Line Cinema he had discussed the possibilities of her playing the part, from the day the rights to the book were sold, to 10 years later when she signed on for the film. As a part of her preparation, Kidman re-read the whole His Dark Materials trilogy, and wrote small notes, whenever she found something personal about the character, before filming began. The film ended up being the biggest film of her career, with a budget of $180 million and a box office income of $372 million worldwide, the biggest live-action success that she has experienced. \n* Studied ballet and drama in Australia. \n* \'Keith Urban (II)\' (qv)\'s brother, Shane Urban, was best man at their wedding. His friend, \'Marlon Holden\' (qv), was the groomsman. \n* \'Keith Urban (II)\' (qv) gave Nicole a first edition of \'Emily Brontë\' (qv)\'s novel, \"Wuthering Heights\", purchased from Sotherby\'s for $200,000 as a wedding present. \n* Wrote the introduction to the book \"Truth: Personas, Needs, and Flaws in the Art of Building Actors and Creating Characters\" by her friend and acting consultant \'Susan Batson (I)\' (qv). \n* 2004: Topped The Hollywood Reporter\'s list of the highest-paid actresses in Hollywood. In 2007, Forbes Magazine estimated her earnings for the year to be $28 million. \n* 9/01: Attended the Royal Premiere of _Moulin Rouge! (2001)_ (qv) alongside \'Kylie Minogue\' (qv), \'Prince Charles\' (qv) and \'Ewan McGregor\' (qv). \n* Is a fan of actress \'Aishwarya Rai Bachchan\' (qv), and her film _Bride & Prejudice (2004)_ (qv). Nicole got to meet Aishwarya for the first time at the 2005 Time Magazine\'s 100 most influential people in the world dinner held in New York City. \n* Was originally cast as Ulla in _The Producers (2005)_ (qv) and was considered for roles in _Nymphomaniac: Vol. I (2013)_ (qv) and _Bel Ami (2012)_ (qv). All three roles eventually went to \'Uma Thurman\' (qv). Furthermore, \'Joel Schumacher\' (qv) also wanted Kidman to play Poison Ivy in _Batman Forever (1995)_ (qv), but the use of the character was delayed to a later entry in the Batman series, in which Thurman played the part. \n* The bridesmaids at her wedding were her adopted daughter Isabella Jane Cruise, her sister \'Antonia Kidman\' (qv) and her niece Lucia. \n* In honor of her June 2006 wedding to singer \'Keith Urban (II)\' (qv), Australian TV presenters \'Simon Reeve (I)\' (qv) and \'Nelson Aspen\' (qv) teamed up to sing Nicole a live rendition of the theme song \"Bewitched\" on the morning news program _\"Sunrise\" (2002)_ (qv). \n* On April 13, 2007, she was awarded the Companion of the Order of Australia, which is Australia\'s top civil honor, at a ceremony in Canberra. She was honored for her contribution to cinema as well as her charity work with women, children and cancer research. \n* Wore a red wig for her role in _Moulin Rouge! (2001)_ (qv). \n* 2006: Presented her _The Peacemaker (1997)_ (qv) co-star \'George Clooney\' (qv) his Best Supporting Actor Oscar. \n* Had several projects lined up during the mid-aughts that never went to fruition; \'Baz Luhrmann\' (qv)\'s doomed Alexander the Great-project with \'Leonardo DiCaprio\' (qv), \"American Darlings\" with \'Jennifer Lopez (I)\' (qv), \'Kar-Wai Wong\' (qv)\'s \"The Lady from Shanghai\", \'Ridley Scott\' (qv)\'s \"Emma\'s War\", \'Paul Verhoeven (I)\' (qv)\'s \"Other Powers\", the \'Steven Spielberg\' (qv)-produced \"The Rivals\" co-starring \'Marion Cotillard\' (qv). She also circled roles in _I Heart Huckabees (2004)_ (qv) and _The Brothers Grimm (2005)_ (qv), but dropped out of both. \n* 2004: Presented her _The Interpreter (2005)_ (qv) co-star \'Sean Penn (I)\' (qv) his Best Actor Oscar. \n* December 2007: According to Forbes, her movies averaged $8 of gross income for every dollar the actress got paid. \n* Earns $4 million per year endorsing Chanel No. 5 perfume. Her three year contract was up for renewal in 2007 and was extended to 2009, where it was concluded. She was replaced by \'Audrey Tautou\' (qv). \n* Turned down the lead role of Roxie Hart in _Chicago (2002)_ (qv) because she had just completed _Moulin Rouge! (2001)_ (qv) and did not want to do two musicals in a row. Her friend, \'Renée Zellweger\' (qv), won the role of Roxie and an Oscar nomination for her efforts. \n* She and husband \'Keith Urban (II)\' (qv), purchased a farm near Leipers Fork, Tennessee, which they are now renovating. (November 2007). \n* Moved in together with \'Jack Black (I)\' (qv) and \'Jennifer Jason Leigh\' (qv) during filming _Margot at the Wedding (2007)_ (qv), because they wanted to perfect their roles as a dysfunctional family. \n* To prepare for her role in _The Human Stain (2003)_ (qv) she visited women\'s shelters and talked to former victims of abuse for inspiration. \n* Her and \'Tom Cruise\' (qv)\'s high profile divorce was finalized the same week that her horror movie _The Others (2001)_ (qv) was released. \n* When she went to Sweden to shoot _Dogville (2003)_ (qv), it was the first time in 15 years that she had flown on a public plane. \n* For her role in _Birthday Girl (2001)_ (qv) she went to the Russian Embassy in Australia for help in speaking Russian. She didn\'t work with any other coach on the set except the woman from the embassy. \n* Got the role as Diane Arbus in _Fur: An Imaginary Portrait of Diane Arbus (2006)_ (qv) after \'Samantha Morton (I)\' (qv) backed out. \n* While filming _The Invasion (2007/I)_ (qv) she was involved in an accident while riding with others in a picture car mounted on trailer being towed by a camera truck rig. While filming a stunt in downtown Los Angeles, the truck took a turn too wide, slid on wet pavement, and collided with a light standard. Several stunt artists on the picture car were injured in the accident. Ms. Kidman was brought to the hospital as a precaution, but was able to return to the set the next day. \n* Did her own singing for _Moulin Rouge! (2001)_ (qv), _Happy Feet (2006)_ (qv) and _Nine (2009)_ (qv). \n* While reaching a divorce from \'Tom Cruise\' (qv), Nicole lived with her friend \'Naomi Watts\' (qv). \n* It was widely claimed that her friend \'Naomi Watts\' (qv) turned down the female lead in _The Interpreter (2005)_ (qv) because she knew Nicole wanted the role. However, the part was actually written exclusively for Kidman, who was a longtime friend of \'Sydney Pollack\' (qv), the director of the film. They previously starred in _Eyes Wide Shut (1999)_ (qv) and he produced her films _Birthday Girl (2001)_ (qv) and _Cold Mountain (2003)_ (qv). \n* Has worked with some of the most celebrated and acclaimed directors in the movie business: \'Stanley Kubrick\' (qv), \'Lars von Trier\' (qv), \'Werner Herzog\' (qv), \'Chan-wook Park (I)\' (qv), \'Noah Baumbach\' (qv), \'Anthony Minghella\' (qv), \'Stephen Daldry\' (qv), \'David Fincher\' (qv), \'Baz Luhrmann\' (qv), \'Jane Campion\' (qv), \'Gus Van Sant\' (qv), \'Ron Howard (I)\' (qv), The Wachowski siblings and \'George Miller (II)\' (qv). In addition to this, she has expressed an eagerness to work with \'Michael Haneke\' (qv) and has always dreamed of working with her friend \'Steven Spielberg\' (qv). \n* One of her favorite collaborators is costume designer \'Ann Roth (I)\' (qv), with whom she worked on five projects including _The Hours (2002)_ (qv) and _Rabbit Hole (2010)_ (qv). \n* Some of her favorite movies are _Gone with the Wind (1939)_ (qv), _Breaking the Waves (1996)_ (qv), _The Wizard of Oz (1939)_ (qv), _Grease (1978)_ (qv), _Rear Window (1954)_ (qv) and _The Way We Were (1973)_ (qv). \n* Good friends with \'Renée Zellweger\' (qv), \'Jim Carrey\' (qv), \'Russell Crowe\' (qv) and \'Meryl Streep\' (qv). She also became good friends with \'Daniel Craig (I)\' (qv) after working with him on _The Invasion (2007/I)_ (qv) and _The Golden Compass (2007)_ (qv). In 2014 she revealed that whenever he goes to Australia, he always stays at her house. \n* She loved wearing the prosthetic nose, that she originally used in _The Hours (2002)_ (qv) and wore it in private too, mainly as she was undergoing a divorce from \'Tom Cruise\' (qv) at the time and was attracting a lot of paparazzi interest. Much to her delight, by wearing her fake nose out and about, she found she could easily evade the paparazzi as they didn\'t recognize her. \n* She decided not to imitate \'Virginia Woolf (I)\' (qv)\'s actual tone and voice in her portrayal in _The Hours (2002)_ (qv), because she feared people thought it would be comic. \n* She is distantly related to Isabel Kidman, the second wife of \'Jack Lee (III)\' (qv). \n* Replaced \'Catherine Zeta-Jones\' (qv) in the role of Claudia in _Nine (2009)_ (qv) after she quit due to scheduling conflicts. Kidman began rehearsals two months after giving birth to her daughter Sunday. \n* Became pregnant twice by \'Tom Cruise\' (qv) during their marriage. In 1991, she suffered an ectopic pregnancy, resulting in their decision to adopt, and then in 2000, prior to their separation, she suffered a miscarriage. \n* Auditioned for the role of Molly Jensen in _Ghost (1990)_ (qv). Director \'Jerry Zucker\' (qv) said in the DVD commentary that he was very impressed with Kidman, but ultimately cast \'Demi Moore\' (qv) because she was a bigger name. The film\'s writer, \'Bruce Joel Rubin\' (qv), was equally impressed and later cast Kidman as the lead in _My Life (1993)_ (qv). \n* Turned down \'Jodie Foster\' (qv)\'s role in _The Brave One (2007)_ (qv). \n* Is very close friends with fellow Aussie \'Hugh Jackman\' (qv), her _Australia (2008)_ (qv) co-star. \n* Was two months pregnant with her daughter Sunday when she completed filming on _Australia (2008)_ (qv). \n* With her performance as Virginia Wolf in _The Hours (2002)_ (qv) lasting around 25 minutes, she has one of the shortest Best Actress Oscar wins in history. \n* Returned to work two months after the birth of her daughter Faith in order to begin filming _Hemingway & Gellhorn (2012) (TV)_ (qv). \n* Owns a massive 3-bedroom, 2-bath river-view apartment in renowned glass tower designed by \'Richard Meier (I)\' (qv) on Greenwich Village\'s Perry Street by Hudson River in Manhattan. \n* Was in a band when she was a teenager, she was the singer. \n* Said that if she had not been an actress, she would have been a doctor. \n* She studied at St. Martin\'s Youth Theatre, Melbourne, Australia; Australian Theatre for Young People, Sydney, Australia and Philip Street Theatre, Sydney, Australia; majored in voice, production and theater history. \n* When she was a child, used to go fishing with her grandfather. \n* Decided to do _Eyes Wide Shut (1999)_ (qv) before reading the script. \n* \'Tom Cruise\' (qv) used to call her \"The Wild Australian\". \n* When \'Stanley Kubrick\' (qv) died, she went to St. Patrick church in New York and lit a candle in his memory. \n* When she married \'Tom Cruise\' (qv), part of their promise was that they would never be separated for more than two weeks. The same promise was applied between her and her second husband \'Keith Urban (II)\' (qv). However, they later reduced it to one week and finally they settled on three days of separation to be the limit. \n* Was nicknamed \"Stalky\" while attending school. \n* Raised in Longueville, Sydney and attended a Catholic school. \n* Shares the services of publicist \'Pat Kingsley\' (qv) with her friend, \'Sandra Bullock\' (qv). \n* Has two adopted children with her first husband \'Tom Cruise\' (qv) - a daughter Isabella Jane Kidman-Cruise (aka Isabella Cruise, born on December 22, 1992) and a son Connor Anthony Kidman-Cruise (aka \'Connor Cruise\' (qv), born on January 17, 1995). \n* When she separated from husband \'Tom Cruise\' (qv) in February 2001, she was 3 months pregnant with what would have been their first biological child, but had a miscarriage. \n* Gave birth to her third child at age 41, a daughter Sunday Rose Kidman-Urban on July 7, 2008. Child\'s father is her second husband, \'Keith Urban (I)\' (qv). \n* Became the mother for the fourth time at age 43, when her daughter Faith Margaret Kidman-Urban was born on December 28, 2010 via surrogate. Child\'s father is her second husband, \'Keith Urban (I)\' (qv). \n* Attended \'Princess Diana\' (qv)\'s funeral with her then-husband, \'Tom Cruise\' (qv). \n* The longest she has gone without an Oscar nomination is 8 years, between _The Hours (2002)_ (qv) and _Rabbit Hole (2010)_ (qv). \n* She serves as an Ambassador of the Sydney Children\'s Hospital, Randwick, and have hosted events for the children, including special screenings of _Happy Feet (2006)_ (qv), _The Golden Compass (2007)_ (qv) and _Paddington (2014)_ (qv). \n* Originally turned down her part in _Stoker (2013)_ (qv), because she wished to spend time with her kids. However, director \'Chan-wook Park (I)\' (qv) was eager to have her play the part of Evelyn that he choose to place setting in Nashville. The movie was filmed five minutes from her home and she began filming a week after wrapping _The Paperboy (2012)_ (qv). \n* On set of _Cold Mountain (2003)_ (qv) she gave co-star \'Natalie Portman\' (qv) the advice to \"Always choose a film by its director. You\'re never certain how the movie will turn out, but you are always guaranteed an interesting experience\". In 2010, Portman said that she always had that in mind, during her career. \n* \'Zac Efron\' (qv) said that shooting the improvised dance scene with her in _The Paperboy (2012)_ (qv), was \"the day he became a man\". \n* She saved \'Hugh Jackman\' (qv) from a poisonous scorpion on the set of _Australia (2008)_ (qv). While she was about to join Hugh in the bag, she noticed the poisonous scorpion crawling up his leg. She calmly told him not to move and squatted down, scooped the arachnid into her hat and walked over to the woods and released it. Everyone applauded but was asked why she hadn\'t just stomped on it. She said, \'I would never kill an animal. Every creature here has its purpose. This one just didn\'t belong in Hugh\'s bag!\'\". \n* As of 2017, has appeared in three films that were nominated for the Best Picture Oscar: _Moulin Rouge! (2001)_ (qv), _The Hours (2002)_ (qv) and _Lion (2016)_ (qv). \n* In both _Dead Calm (1989)_ (qv) and _Rabbit Hole (2010)_ (qv) Nicole played the mother of a boy named Danny. Danny was killed in car accidents in both films. \n* Developed shortsightedness in 1998 and had laser treatment to rectify the problem. Usually wears contact lenses but wears glasses on occasions. \n* Godmother of \'Simon Baker (I)\' (qv) and \'Rebecca Riggs (I)\' (qv)\' children Henry and Claude and of \'Rupert Murdoch\' (qv)\'s daughters Grace and Chloe. \n* 12 films in her career have been nominated for at least one Academy Award. The most common categories among her representative films are \'Best Supporting Actress\' and \'Best Costume Design\', for which five of her films have been nominated. \n* In October 2012, she was honored with a Gala event at the 50th-annual New York Film Festival. \n* She attended the opening ceremony of the London Summer Olympics, with husband \'Keith Urban (II)\' (qv), due to her status of spokeswoman for Omega Watches, the official time keeper for the Olympic Games. \n* In September 2013, she attended the announcement of Qingdao Oriental Movie Metropolis in Qingdao, China. She was honored as \"Best Global Film Actress\" at the same event. \'Jackie Chan (I)\' (qv) presented her the honor. \n* Spent several weeks in Kununurra, Western Australia, filming _Australia (2008)_ (qv) with temperatures soaring to 109 °F. \n* Attended the world premiere of her friend \'Naomi Watts\' (qv)\'s first major studio film, _The Ring (2002)_ (qv), next to Watts and \'Heath Ledger\' (qv) at the Mann Bruin Theatre, Westwood, CA. \n* Served member of the main competition jury at the 2013 Cannes Film Festival. \n* Has starred in two films that were selected as the opening film at the Cannes Film Festival: _Moulin Rouge! (2001)_ (qv) and _Grace of Monaco (2014)_ (qv). \n* The Valentino dress she wore at the red carpet event of _Nebraska (2013)_ (qv) at Cannes Film Festival, was the dress that \'Anne Hathaway\' (qv) originally was meant to wear at the The 85th Annual Academy Awards. Hathaway discarded the dress at the last minute, because it was too similar to \'Amanda Seyfried (I)\' (qv)\'s dress. \n* Was a longtime friend (25 years) of \'L\'Wren Scott\' (qv). \n* Her Hawaiian name is Hokulani, which means \"heavenly star\". She was named after a baby elephant, that lived at the local zoo of her childhood home, as her mother loves elephants. \n* \'Sofía Vergara\' (qv) was originally cast as Charlotte Bless in _The Paperboy (2012)_ (qv), the role that Kidman eventually assumed. Vergara herself has said Kidman was a better fit for the part. \n* Often teaches herself various skills for different roles. Among them, she knows how to twirl and throw knives, due to her role in _Paddington (2014)_ (qv), for which she also took a taxidermy class as part of her preparation. \n* \'Frank Ocean\' (qv) sampled one of her monologues from _Eyes Wide Shut (1999)_ (qv) into his song \"Love Crimes\", released on his debut album \"Nostalgia, Ultra\" from 2011. \n* Both of her blockbusters from 2007, _The Invasion (2007/I)_ (qv) and _The Golden Compass (2007)_ (qv), went through massive creative changes during post-production, due to demand from the studios. The interference left both the directors publicly unsatisfied with the final results of their respective films. A director\'s cut edition has never been released of either films. \n* Was the 123rd actress to receive an Academy Award; she won the Best Actress Oscar for _The Hours (2002)_ (qv) at _The 75th Annual Academy Awards (2003) (TV)_ (qv) on March 23, 2003. \n* Producer \'Harvey Weinstein\' (qv) suggested her and \'Judi Dench\' (qv) to play Barbara and Violet in _August: Osage County (2013)_ (qv), during the initial casting process of the film. However, the screenwriter of the film, \'Tracy Letts\' (qv), voted against the idea, saying that the two roles should be played by American actors. \n* Sharks are one of her passions and to shark-dive is one of her favorite activities. She considers sharks to be \"beautiful\". \n* Was attached to produce and star as Einar Wegener in _The Danish Girl (2015)_ (qv) for several years and she considered it to be a passion project for her. She revealed in 2010, that at one point, following difficulties with finding a director, she considered making it her directorial debut. However, in 2014, the project moved forward again, but without Kidman\'s involvement. \n* Has a skydiving license. \n* \'Meryl Streep\' (qv) considers her to be one of the best actresses of her generation. \'Emily Blunt\' (qv), \'Freida Pinto\' (qv), \'Dakota Johnson\' (qv) and \'Sophia Bush\' (qv) all considers her among their favorite actresses. \n* When she was 14, she got her first job at the Capitol Theatre in Sydney, where she worked as an usherette at Sesame Street Live. \n* She and former husband \'Tom Cruise\' (qv) were offered guest roles in _\"The Simpsons\" (1989)_ (qv) in the episode \"When You Dish Upon A Star\". They turned it down and were replaced by then-couple \'Alec Baldwin (I)\' (qv) and \'Kim Basinger\' (qv). \n* She was originally reluctant to star in _The Others (2001)_ (qv), since it explored such dark places. She tried to persuade the director \'Alejandro Amenábar\' (qv) and the Weinstein brothers, the producers of the film, to find another actress for the part and even suggested \'Julianne Moore\' (qv) to the Weinsteins. However, in 2010, Kidman revealed that the film is one of her favorites among her own filmography. \n* The on-location dressing room she used for _Cold Mountain (2003)_ (qv) was an old converted bus once owned by executed Romanian dictator Nicolae Ceausescu. \n* Owns alpacas at her ranch in Nashville. \n* Was a part of the notoriously secret and long shoot of _Eyes Wide Shut (1999)_ (qv). For one minute of footage of the film, director \'Stanley Kubrick\' (qv) demanded her to do shoot six days of naked sex scenes with a male model. Not only did he ask the pair to pose in over 50 erotic positions, he also banned her then-husband and co-star \'Tom Cruise\' (qv) from the set and forbade her to assuage her husband\'s tension by telling him what happened during the shoot. The decision to direct each actor separately an forbid them to share notes was done to exaggerate the distrust between their fictional husband and wife. \n* Was close friends with \'Lauren Bacall\' (qv). She considered Bacall to be her \"American Mother\". \n* Noted comedienne \'Joan Rivers\' (qv) considered her to be one of the most intelligent and funniest actress in Hollywood. She also considered her to be among \"the last real movie stars\". \n* (September 2014) Her father, Dr. Antony \"Tony\" Kidman, died of a heart attack in Singapore at the age of 75. Dr. Kidman had been declared as deceased by Tan Tock Seng Hospital at 9:54 AM SGT. He is survived by, among others, his wife (Janelle, with whom he celebrated a Golden Anniversary in January), children, in-law children, and 10 grandchildren. \n* In December 2007, she was paid \"substantial\" damages by the British news paper Daily Telegraph, after they wrongly accused her of having breached provisions in her promotional contract for Chanel No. 5 perfume. In the article \"Chanel Left Fuming\", they reported that she carried a bottle of a rival fragrance at the premiere of _The Golden Compass (2007)_ (qv). Kidman\'s lawyer, John Kelly, said his client suffered significant embarrassment and distress over the fabricated Telegraph story, The newspaper later determined that its source for the story had made it up. David Price, the Telegraph\'s solicitor-advocate, apologized on behalf of the paper and agreed to pay Kidman\'s legal costs as well as substantial undisclosed damages, which she donated to Unifem, the United Nations Development Fund for Women. \n* In 2013, both she and husband \'Keith Urban (II)\' (qv) were nominated for a Golden Globe. She received her tenth nomination, while he received his first. \n* She was well-respected by the late and famed film-critic \'Roger Ebert\' (qv). He considered her to have a dichotomous career; \"She seems to be two people: the glamorous star of \"Moulin Rouge\" and \"Nine,\" and the risky, daring actress in \"Birth,\" \"The Hours\" and \"Eyes Wide Shut.\" He also believed that in time, she will gain more respect; \"Celebrity has clouded her image; if she were less glamorous, she would be more praised. Age will only be an asset to her.\". \n* In 2014 she was honored at the Shanghai Film Festival with an \"Outstanding Contribution to Film Industry Award\". \'Hugh Grant (I)\' (qv) presented the award to her at the event. \n* She cites Austrailian and South African accents as being the two most difficult dialects in English. \n* Her Oscar is placed over the fireplace in her mother\'s home. \n* Infamously, she made her debut on _\"Jimmy Kimmel Live!\" (2003)_ (qv) by giving host \'Jimmy Kimmel\' (qv) a brief lap-dance. \n* Became a viral hit in January, 2015 following an appearance on _\"The Tonight Show Starring Jimmy Fallon\" (2014)_ (qv), in which they discuss an embarrassing encounter between the two. The clip is said to have gained 55 million views online during its first week, including 18 million views on YouTube. \n* Her performance in \'Sam Mendes (I)\' (qv)\'s West End play \"The Blue Room\" (1999) inspired both \'Baz Luhrmann\' (qv) to cast her in _Moulin Rouge! (2001)_ (qv) and \'Stephen Daldry\' (qv) to cast her in _The Hours (2002)_ (qv). She received an Oscar nomination for both films. \n* As of 2017, three of her films _Australia (2008)_ (qv), _Happy Feet (2006)_ (qv) and _Lion (2016)_ (qv) all ranks among the top five of the highest grossing Australian film productions at the box office of her native Australia. She is the only performer to achieve this feat. Her film _Moulin Rouge! (2001)_ (qv) also ranks as #6 on the same chart, while _Lion (2016)_ (qv) is the highest grossing independent Australian film of all time. \n* Has portrayed the villain in two adaptations of British children literature that features a talking bear as one of the main characters: _The Golden Compass (2007)_ (qv) and _Paddington (2014)_ (qv). The two characters also shares the initials of M.C. - Marisa Coulter and Millicent Clyde. \n* To date (2015) she\'s been taller than both of her husbands and older than one. \n* Is one of 27 actresses to have received an Academy Award nomination for their performance in a musical; hers being _Moulin Rouge! (2001)_ (qv). The others, in chronological order, are: \'Bessie Love\' (qv) for _The Broadway Melody (1929)_ (qv), \'Grace Moore (I)\' (qv) for _One Night of Love (1934)_ (qv), \'Jean Hagen\' (qv) for _Singin\' in the Rain (1952)_ (qv), \'Marjorie Rambeau\' (qv) for _Torch Song (1953)_ (qv), \'Dorothy Dandridge\' (qv) for _Carmen Jones (1954)_ (qv), \'Deborah Kerr (I)\' (qv) for _The King and I (1956)_ (qv), \'Rita Moreno (I)\' (qv) for _West Side Story (1961)_ (qv), \'Gladys Cooper (I)\' (qv) for _My Fair Lady (1964)_ (qv), \'Julie Andrews (I)\' (qv) for _Mary Poppins (1964)_ (qv), _The Sound of Music (1965)_ (qv), and _Victor Victoria (1982)_ (qv), \'Debbie Reynolds (I)\' (qv) for _The Unsinkable Molly Brown (1964)_ (qv), \'Peggy Wood (I)\' (qv) for _The Sound of Music (1965)_ (qv), \'Carol Channing\' (qv) for _Thoroughly Modern Millie (1967)_ (qv), \'Kay Medford\' (qv) for _Funny Girl (1968)_ (qv), \'Barbra Streisand\' (qv) for _Funny Girl (1968)_ (qv), \'Liza Minnelli\' (qv) for _Cabaret (1972)_ (qv), \'Ronee Blakley\' (qv) for _Nashville (1975)_ (qv), \'Lily Tomlin\' (qv) for _Nashville (1975)_ (qv), \'Ann-Margret\' (qv) for _Tommy (1975)_ (qv), \'Lesley Ann Warren\' (qv) for _Victor Victoria (1982)_ (qv), \'Amy Irving (I)\' (qv) for _Yentl (1983)_ (qv), \'Queen Latifah\' (qv) for _Chicago (2002)_ (qv), \'Catherine Zeta-Jones\' (qv) for _Chicago (2002)_ (qv), \'Renée Zellweger\' (qv) for _Chicago (2002)_ (qv), \'Jennifer Hudson (I)\' (qv) for _Dreamgirls (2006)_ (qv), \'Penélope Cruz\' (qv) for _Nine (2009)_ (qv), \'Anne Hathaway\' (qv) for _Les Misérables (2012)_ (qv), \'Meryl Streep\' (qv) for _Into the Woods (2014)_ (qv), and \'Emma Stone (III)\' (qv) for _La La Land (2016/I)_ (qv). \n* Is one of 20 actresses who did not receive an Oscar nomination for their Best Actress in a Comedy/Musical Golden Globe-winning performance; hers being for _To Die For (1995)_ (qv). The others, in chronological order, are: \'June Allyson\' (qv) for _Too Young to Kiss (1951)_ (qv), \'Ethel Merman\' (qv) for _Call Me Madam (1953)_ (qv), \'Jean Simmons (I)\' (qv) for _Guys and Dolls (1955)_ (qv), \'Taina Elg\' (qv) and \'Kay Kendall (I)\' (qv) for _Les Girls (1957)_ (qv), \'Marilyn Monroe\' (qv) for _Some Like It Hot (1959)_ (qv), \'Rosalind Russell (I)\' (qv) for _A Majority of One (1961)_ (qv) and _Gypsy (1962)_ (qv), \'Patty Duke\' (qv) for _Me, Natalie (1969)_ (qv), \'Twiggy (I)\' (qv) for _The Boy Friend (1971)_ (qv), \'Raquel Welch\' (qv) for _The Three Musketeers (1973)_ (qv), \'Barbra Streisand\' (qv) for _A Star Is Born (1976)_ (qv), \'Bernadette Peters\' (qv) for _Pennies from Heaven (1981)_ (qv), \'Kathleen Turner (I)\' (qv) for _Romancing the Stone (1984)_ (qv) and _Prizzi\'s Honor (1985)_ (qv), \'Miranda Richardson (I)\' (qv) for _Enchanted April (1991)_ (qv), \'Jamie Lee Curtis\' (qv) for _True Lies (1994)_ (qv), \'Madonna (I)\' (qv) for _Evita (1996)_ (qv), \'Renée Zellweger\' (qv) for _Nurse Betty (2000)_ (qv), \'Sally Hawkins (I)\' (qv) for _Happy-Go-Lucky (2008)_ (qv), and \'Amy Adams (III)\' (qv) for _Big Eyes (2014/I)_ (qv). \n* Holds the all time record of being the actress to have appeared on most covers for the fashion magazine Vogue (US edition): 8 times. \n* In 2009, she was among four Oscar-winning Australian actors to be immortalized onto postage stamps in their home country. Each of the actors has two stamps, showing them both in and out of character. \n* The filming process of _Moulin Rouge! (2001)_ (qv) was halted for two weeks in November 1999 after she fractured two ribs and injured her knee while rehearsing a dance routine for the film. Many of the scenes of the film, where she is seen only from the chest up, including \"A real actress!\", were shot while she was in a wheelchair. \n* Eldest daughter Sunday Rose was born two weeks early at 9:00 am Central daylight time on July 7th 2008 weighing 2.934186 Kilos or 6 lbs 7.5 oz at birth. \n* _Bewitched (2005)_ (qv) was the first movie among her own filmography, that was seen by her two youngest daughters, Sunday and Faith. However, due to their young age, the two girls believed their mother was able to perform magic tricks, and were left confused and disappointing when they realized the truth. \n* Spent four and a half years developing _Rabbit Hole (2010)_ (qv). It marked her first movie as both leading actress and producer on a film. \n* Her fans refers to themselves as \"Kidmaniacs\". \n* In 2015, she starred as Rosalind Franklin, the English chemist and X-ray crystallographer, in the play \"Photograph 51\", as a tribute to her father, the late Antony Kidman, who worked as a biochemist and clinical psychologist. She donated her entire salary to The Actors\' Benevolent Fund, and in her father\'s name to King\'s College, in honor of her father and Rosalind Franklin. \n* Has worked with producer \'Harvey Weinstein\' (qv) on seven projects. \n* Has co-starred opposite several up-and-coming actors in their feature film debut, such as \'Thandie Newton\' (qv), \'Miles Teller\' (qv), \'Dakota Blue Richards\' (qv), \'Maddison Brown (I)\' (qv), \'Brandon Walters (II)\' (qv) and \'Sunny Pawar\' (qv). \n* Despite her singing skills, she has ruled out the possibility of ever making an official duet with husband \'Keith Urban (II)\' (qv). However, they did once perform together in the public, at G\'Day Gala, where they reworked \"Down Under\" by \'Men at Work (II)\' (qv) as a tribute to \'Simon Baker (I)\' (qv). \n* Learned to ride camels for her role in _Queen of the Desert (2015)_ (qv) and on set she had her own male dromedary, named Barbie. \n* Spoofed by \'Jennifer Saunders (I)\' (qv) and \'Patsy Kensit\' (qv) on _\"French and Saunders\" (1987)_ (qv). \n* Was personally handpicked by \'Julia Roberts (I)\' (qv) to be her co-star of _Secret in Their Eyes (2015)_ (qv). Roberts sent her an email going; \"Will you do this with me?\". \n* Won the Evening Standard Theatre Award for her performance as Rosalind Franklin in \"Photograph 51\". \'Jeremy Irons\' (qv) presented her the award. \n* \'Cate Blanchett\' (qv) considers her a trailblazer for Australian actors having a chance making a career in the US. \n* She was nominated for the 2016 Laurence Olivier Theatre Award as \'Best Actress in a Play\' (season 2015) for her performance in \"Photograph 51\". \n* She has worked with 4 directors who have won a Best Director Oscar: \'Robert Benton (I)\' (qv), \'Ron Howard (I)\' (qv), \'Anthony Minghella\' (qv), and \'Sydney Pollack\' (qv). \n* Was personally handpicked by Sue Brierley to portray her in _Lion (2016)_ (qv). Kidman bonded with her young co-star \'Sunny Pawar\' (qv) by playing cricket with him - scenes which eventually made it into the final film. \n* Celebrated her 20th birthday on the set of _Dead Calm (1989)_ (qv). \n* Experienced her very first romantic kiss while watching _The Shining (1980)_ (qv) with a boyfriend. \n* She is friends with and a big fan of James Wan. \n* She has been in two different film adaptations of DC Comics\' characters: Batman Forever (1995) and Aquaman (2018). \n* In 1999 she was wanted for The Adaptive Ultimate which was to be directed by Jan De Bont for Twentieth Century Fox but her schedule was full and Fox wanted it into production asap. \n* Friends with \'Russell Crowe\' (qv), \'Renée Zellweger\' (qv), \'George Clooney\' (qv), \'Halle Berry\' (qv), and \'Kristin Chenoweth\' (qv). \n* \'Sofia Coppola\' (qv) wanted her to play a Wicked Sea Witch in live-action adaptation of \'Hans Christian Andersen\' (qv)\'s fairy tale \"The Little Mermaid\", which was set up at Universal Pictures. The project fell apart, but Kidman later played a Mermaid Warrior Queen in _Aquaman (2018)_ (qv). \n* Made two films back-to-back with \'Colin Farrell (I)\' (qv), _The Killing of a Sacred Deer (2017)_ (qv) and _The Beguiled (2017)_ (qv). Both films played in competition at Cannes Film Festival, where the first was awarded by the festival jury for its screenplay by \'Yorgos Lanthimos\' (qv) and the latter for its director \'Sofia Coppola\' (qv). \n* Joined the second season of _\"Top of the Lake\" (2013)_ (qv) after professional negations and scheduling issues which took a full year to accommodate. The part was written with Kidman in mind and re-teams her with \'Jane Campion\' (qv), making it their third collaboration together after _The Portrait of a Lady (1996)_ (qv) and _In the Cut (2003)_ (qv). Kidman was 14 years old and in a play when she was discovered by Campion, who offered her a part in her student film _A Girl\'s Own Story (1986)_ (qv). Kidman turned it down due to explicit content that she found uncomfortable at the time. \n* Awarded a specially-created \'70th Anniversary Prize\' at Cannes Film Festival in 2017 for her contribution to cinema. She had four projects screened at the festival that year, including two in competition. The anniversary award is given once every decade and Kidman is the first woman to receive the award. \n* In _The Beguiled (2017)_ (qv) a sponge scene with her and Colin Farrell required several takes, as the afternoon light inflicted the cinematography. By the time the scene wrapped, Kidman had sponged Farrell for almost two hours. \n* Her prenup with Keith Urban has the following stipulations: 1. for every year they\'re married, Urban gets $640,000, 2. if he should relapse in drug use, the contract becomes null and void and he gets nothing. \n* Has been tied up and had her mouth gagged with tape in three separate movies: _Batman Forever (1995)_ (qv), _Birthday Girl (2001)_ (qv) and _The Killing of a Sacred Deer (2017)_ (qv). \n* Was considered for the dual lead role in _Passion of Mind (2000)_ (qv). \n\n* (1983) Appeared as an extra in the video clip of the Pat Wilson song \"Bop Girl\". \n* (9-10/1998) Stage: \"The Blue Room\", Donmar Warehouse, London, England. \n* (2001) Hit single with \'Robbie Williams (I)\' (qv), \'Something Stupid\'. Also appeared in a music video for the song. \n* (2001)Sings on two volumes of soundtrack CDs for Moulin Rouge \n* (2002)TV commercial for El Corte Inglés directed by \'Alejandro Amenábar\' (qv). \n* (2004) TV commercial for Chanel N°5 perfume, directed by \'Baz Luhrmann\' (qv). \n* (2003) Print ad for Womens Cancer Research Fund. \n* (2007) TV commercial for Nintendo. \n* (2008) Print ads for Omega. \n* (2009) French TV commercial for Schweppes, filmed in India with Arjun Rampar and Rubina Ali, directed by Shekhar Kapur. \n* (2010) TV commercial for Schweppes. \n* (2011) Print advertisement for OMEGA (Ladymatic) Swiss watches (UK) \n* (2012) Recorded the audio-book of \"To the Lighthouse\" by Virginia Woolf . \n* (2015) TV commercial for Etihad Airways \n* (2015) TV commercials for compare the market.com \n* (5 September - 21 November 2015) Stage play: \"Photograph 51\" - playing Rosalind Franklin opposite \'Stephen Campbell Moore\' (qv) (Maurice Wilkins), \'Will Attenborough\' (qv) (James Watson), \'Edward Bennett (IV)\' (qv) (Francis Crick), \'Patrick Kennedy (IV)\' (qv) (Don Caspar), Joshua Silver (Ray Gosling). Written by Anna Ziegler. Directed by \'Michael Grandage\' (qv) (Noel Coward Theatre London) \n* (2015) TV commercial for Swisse vitamins \n\n* Michael Kohler. _Nicole Kidman._ Berlin, Germany: Bertz + Fischer, 2004. ISBN 392947042X \n* David Thomson. _Nicole Kidman._ New York: Alfred A. Knopf, 2006. ISBN 1400042739 \n\n* [August 2000, on her marriage to \'Tom Cruise\' (qv)] Every day there is a compromise. Living with somebody requires a lot of understanding. But I love being married. I really love it. Sometimes I try to downplay it a bit because people are like, \"God you guys!\" I just feel so fortunate that I have found someone who will put up with me and stay with me. \n* [on husband \'Tom Cruise\' (qv)] I wouldn\'t want to be married to me, but luckily he does. \n* [8/01, commenting on her break-up with \'Tom Cruise\' (qv)] Now I can wear heels. \n* It\'s so bizarre, I\'m not scared of snakes or spiders. But I\'m scared of butterflies. There is something eerie about them. Something weird! \n* [on winning the Academy Award for _The Hours (2002)_ (qv)] And I am standing in front of my mother, and my whole life I have wanted to make my mother proud. And now I\'m going to make my daughter proud. \n* When I heard about the Suzanne role in _To Die For (1995)_ (qv), I thought, \"I\'ll never get it - it\'ll be offered to someone else.\" So I called Gus [\'Gus Van Sant\' (qv)] at home, and he took my call, thank God. I told him I\'d seen _Drugstore Cowboy (1989)_ (qv), and I really wanted to work with him. I said I was destined to work with him. \n* I think it\'s important that we don\'t all have to hold our heads high all the time saying everything\'s fine. \n* These different people that I play become the loves of my life. \n* The split [from \'Tom Cruise\' (qv)] left me very fragile but I\'d love to marry again. \n* I would love to have boobs and a butt like \'Jennifer Lopez (I)\' (qv) but I\'m not having surgery so there it is. \n* [on receiving her star on the Walk of Fame] I\'ve never been so excited to have people walk all over me for the rest of my life. \n* You want to take some responsibility in your choices so that it sets the groundwork for that next generation of actresses - so for me working with \'Lars von Trier\' (qv), I would hope that says to another generation: \"go and seek out those directors, it\'s ok\" \n* [on _Dogville (2003)_ (qv)] One day it would be a fairy tale, the next it was a nightmare. Lars [\'Lars von Trier\' (qv)] was gentle with me - he was gentle and soft, then he would beat me up emotionally when he felt he needed that. I did not always register what was happening until afterwards but you shouldn\'t have too much awareness as an actor, I don\'t think. \n* I have a boy\'s body. I would prefer to have more curves because I think that\'s more beautiful. I would much rather have J. Lo\'s [\'Jennifer Lopez (I)\' (qv)] body than mine. \n* [on filming _The Interpreter (2005)_ (qv) at UN headquarters in New York] As a backdrop for a thriller, it\'s fantastic, but also since I\'m Australian and I\'ve always worked internationally and this is an international place in New York, I really like the kind of communication it represents. I know I sound very much like my character now, but I do believe in this place. \n* [on _Cold Mountain (2003)_ (qv), _The Human Stain (2003)_ (qv) and _Dogville (2003)_ (qv) being released within months of each other] It\'s weird because they\'re all coming out at once. But I made them over 2½ years. \n* You look at somebody\'s work as an actor and you can see their emotional life being fed into it and you can kind of feel them through it. That\'s far more interesting than anything I could say about where I\'m at or who I\'m with. It\'s good to have a little distance. If you discuss your love too much, it just damages it. \n* [on the troubled _The Stepford Wives (2004)_ (qv)] It\'s a comedy. We hope. \n* Even from a very early age I knew I didn\'t want to miss out on anything life had to offer just because it might be considered dangerous. \n* Cinema is a director\'s medium, so you\'re saying, \"What do you want?\" Being an actor is about adapting - physically and emotionally. If that means you have to look great for it and they can make you look great, then thank you. And if you have to have everything washed away, then I\'m willing to do that too. \n* [on _Birth (2004)_ (qv)] This is a film about love. What is a great love? Is there a love of our life? Do you ever recover from the loss of somebody that was so important to you? \n* \'Stanley Kubrick\' (qv) taught me to believe in myself artistically. I spent my 20s raising my children, and wanting to, and being married. That was my driving force. And then he said to me, \"No, you have to respect your talent, and give it some space, and give it some time. Which was a lovely thing to be given. And my children were a little older then. \n* It was by chance that _The Hours (2002)_ (qv) came along. Was I in a place where I could say, \"I\'m going to go to England and make this?\" Yes. Could I do that earlier, when I was married? No, I couldn\'t travel like that. We had a thing where we couldn\'t be separated for more than two weeks. So that made a lot of work just not possible. Which was fine by me. \n* I have moments where I\'ve said, \"Don\'t tread on that crack in the pavement, don\'t have a black cat walk in front of you.\" Deep down am I superstitious? No. Do I believe in trying to be as kind as possible and as compassionate as possible because ultimately you\'re alone with yourself and your own conscience, and you want that to be as clear as possible? That\'s not superstition. You have to just try and stay pure and know what you value. \n* Usually, a young actress can\'t deliver because she doesn\'t have the emotional baggage, really, to play those things. That\'s something that\'s very beautiful about becoming a woman, and becoming a woman in your 30s. If you\'ve lived your life, and lived it where you\'ve said, \"I want to be a participator and not a voyeur\", then you have an enormous amount to pull on. \n* I\'m still just finding my way through. I don\'t actually see a path in front of me. I can see not ever doing it again, and I can also see other things pulling me away from this. It\'s strange, because I know it\'s in my blood in terms of having to somehow act or express myself creatively, but I\'m willing to do it different ways if need be. And I think that\'s partly because when I went through my divorce I dealt with the idea of never ever working again, and never being here and never able to be an actress, and went through an enormous amount of soul searching, and at that time, I was very ready to give it all up, and dealt with that emotionally. I was going, \"Well, I\'ll never be able to do this again.\" And that was OK. And, strangely, as life is so strange, that was when everything exploded. \n* I never feel like I\'m in control. There\'s a certain type of actor that relinquishes control when they act, and then there\'s another type who ends up being a producer and director and they\'re more someone that likes to take control. I fall in the first category, where I like to relinquish control, and fit into somebody else\'s world. And that\'s just lately, but you never feel like you are making choices. You feel like they\'re finding you in a strange way. That\'s why when people say, \"What role do you want to play next?\", I say, \"I don\'t know\". I never know. It\'s about responding to things rather than planning. \n* I think someone said my career defies all logic [laughs]. Because I choose the sort of strange little films, and somehow they\'re the things that make my career. \n* So if you talk about a box-office career, then I\'m a disaster. But somehow, you know, I still manage to find my way to work. \n* I think actors are getting so much more power these days, but I\'m not. I stay very much away from the decisions, the way in which things are orchestrated, what\'s been changed. I just try to stay completely in the role as the actor and as the character. \n* I\'m at a time of my life now where, for me to want to go back and work, it\'d have to be something that I really feel passionately about. \n* Regrets are ridiculous, so I don\'t regret, no. \n* [talking about her character from _Batman Forever (1995)_ (qv)] Chase is attracted to the darker side of life. Batman is very appealing to her. \n* To be an actor you have to have a certain amount of madness in you. That\'s why, when people meet you and you seem very together, they are quite surprised--they don\'t see you behind closed doors. \n* [speaking of her father Antony Kidman] He\'s a great father, I can call him at three in the morning and he\'s there for me. \n* By the time I was a teenager, I had developed skills as a writer, and my father encouraged me to think about a career in journalism. I began keeping a diary, which I maintain to this day. I used to fill whole notebooks with my writings. \n* My parents thought it was nice to develop my imagination, but they never seriously thought that anything would ever come of it. They said that I couldn\'t be an actress because I would be taller than all my leading men, so I thought I would be a writer instead. \n* It was very natural for me to want to disappear into dark theater, I am really very shy. That is something that people never seem to fully grasp because, when you are an actor, you are meant to be an exhibitionist. \n* Do you know I\'m always scared that one day I\'ll look back and say \"God they were the best years of my life and now what?\" There are moments when you feel as if you have been blessed for a while, moments when you think this is perfect, moments when you start to believe that even for an hour, even for a year, it might all happen. So I\'m determined to keep making it get better and better. \n* It\'s a very brave thing to fall in love. You have to be willing to trust somebody else with your whole being, and that\'s very difficult, really difficult and very brave. \n* [about her first role at the age of five] I was one of those terrible kids who said everyone\'s lines. \n* Since I have fair skin, I have to stay out of the sun. I can\'t stand the sun. I dyed my hair red for a while during the 1990s but I\'m actually a natural blonde. \n* I\'m very close to my sister, Antonia [\'Antonia Kidman\' (qv)]. Every day we swim together. I love my sister. \n* When I was a child, I was a natural towhead. Now my hair is naturally a darker shade of blonde. \n* I\'d like to be wise. You have to go through a lot to get there, but I\'m willing to go through a lot. \n* What\'s the point of doing something good if nobody\'s watching? \n* Even from a very early age, I knew I didn\'t want to miss out on anything life had to offer just because it might be considered dangerous. \n* I believe that as much as you take, you have to give back. It\'s important not to focus on yourself too much. \n* I love acting but I don\'t like all of the other stuff associated with it. The interest in celebrities, the press, the Internet, when your identity becomes mixed up in the way people are perceiving you. \n* I have a little bit of a belly, a tiny bit of pooch. It\'s the one thing I don\'t want to lose. I just like having some softness. If I lose that, then Tom [husband \'Tom Cruise\' (qv)] might leave me. \n* I never knew I\'d be in a musical, let alone win an award for one. \n* There\'s no drugs, no Tom [\'Tom Cruise\' (qv)] in a dress, no psychiatrists. \n* Having gone through all of this, I feel in some ways calmer now. It\'s strange, but sad. I think I\'ll always be sad. \n* I love acting, but it\'s much more fun taking the kids to the zoo. \n* [on her split with \'Tom Cruise\' (qv)] My life collapsed. People ran from me because suddenly it was, \"Oh my God! It\'s over for her now!\" \n* I love working with people who are inspired and obsessive. \n* As a child my hair was naturally red, but since I was 13 my hair turned light blonde naturally. It\'s really strange but it happened. Ever since Chase through the night, the directors made me dye my hair red, as they all thought it suited me at the time. Even for _Dead Calm (1989)_ (qv), \'Phillip Noyce\' (qv) made me dye my hair red. Now the directors and studios just let me keep my natural blonde hair. \n* For an actor, facial expressions and emotions are really important. That\'s why I\'ll never have Botox. I\'ve always been against that and seeing Botox on TV with all the swelling and pain put me off it anyway. The directors always allow actors with Botox but I just say. \"No way, not for me\". Drinking lots of water, eating fruit and doing yoga is what keeps me looking young naturally. I swear by it. I also use creams with natural ingredients to make wrinkles less visible. Everybody should try these things rather than going the plastic route, which I just hate. \n* You don\'t have to be naked to be sexy. \n* I\'m a person that carries everything that happened to me in my past, with me into the future. I refuse to let it make me bitter. I still completely believe in love and I remain open to anything that will happen to me. \n* If I packaged toothpaste and told you you were gonna get half the toothpaste in the tube, you probably wouldn\'t buy it. \n* It would be far easier to go, \"Oh, I wish I loved women,\" but I don\'t. I love the way a man thinks. I love the way a man smells. I love the way men look. And I\'m hooked on the male physique - hooked on it. \n* You\'re either going to walk through life and experience it fully or you\'re going to be a voyeur. And I\'m not a voyeur. \n* On living in Nashville: It\'s the warmest, loveliest community I\'ve ever set foot in. For me, it\'s the perfect place to live. To me, it\'s the best part of America...It\'s the easiest place to live. Keith\'s lived there for 20 years. The country music community is very tight. I like the polite nature of it. No traffic. I\'m a big Southern girl now. It\'s just suits me. \n* [on _Hemingway & Gellhorn (2012) (TV)_ (qv)] I knew nothing about Martha, but I\'ve always been drawn to unique women who are willing to take on the world. The exciting thing about this film is that you see her discovering her nature. At the beginning, she\'s a lot of talk. She knows that she\'s either got to get her hands dirty and become what she pretends to be or she\'s a fraud. In the end, Gellhorn out-Hemingways Hemingway. \n* [on music] I\'m so eclectic and I use it in my work all the time. What I used a lot with Lee on _The Paperboy (2012)_ (qv) was \'Al Green (II)\' (qv). Lee has never listened to anything past 1970! \n* [on _The Golden Compass (2007)_ (qv)] I\'m not a huge fan of fantasy. I\'ve always been drawn to more - in terms of filmmaking - I\'ve been drawn to more psychological dramas but I think what drew me to this was it had the intricacies of the characters allow strong performances and that\'s what I found compelling about it. \n* I know the feeling of polarizing films, that make people angry and uncomfortable and I\'m very comfortable with that. \n* [on _Birth (2004)_ (qv)] It created a lot of controversy because of a bathtub scene [with me] and a 10-year-old boy. But I never saw the film that way. It\'s fascinating when choose roles how you perceive them and how they\'re perceived on the outside. \n* I\'ll never forget sort of \"DJ-ing\" after [the Cannes screening of] _Moulin Rouge! (2001)_ (qv). I think there is a photo of me with headphones on, it was with \'Fatboy Slim\' (qv) and the place was going crazy. I hold that dear to my heart. \n* I remember having really mixed reactions to different films I brought to [Cannes Film Festival]. One of them was the Von Trier film, one of them was _To Die For (1995)_ (qv). It was actually an extraordinary reaction for _To Die For (1995)_ (qv). We were not in competition, and I remember being terribly scared that people were gonna hate it, but people loved it. That was very, very instrumentally beginning my career in terms of serious film-making. The critics got behind me on that film and that kind of took me from being \'Tom Cruise\' (qv)\'s wife into being something different, which artistically was such a joy for me. \n* My ability to escape myself probably comes from the riskier the role. It took me a while to learn that and to understand that. I\'ve tried to comfort and play the girl-next-door and the less complicated supporting roles. And for whatever reason I can not latch on, I don\'t quite know how to do it. So the more complicated, the better for me. I don\'t know what that says about me. \n* [on not being Oscar-nominated for _To Die For (1995)_ (qv)] I got a telegram from \'Sean Penn (I)\' (qv) for that, and it said \"You were robbed\". That was all he sent me. And it was as good as an Academy Award nomination. \n* [on _The Golden Compass (2007)_ (qv)]: I love that it\'s a little girl who\'s the protagonist, the hero of the piece, and also that it is about friendship, loyalty and honesty. \n* [on _Australia (2008)_ (qv)]: It was the best experience I\'ve ever had working on a film. \n* [on \'Charlize Theron\' (qv)] I love that we\'re both so tall. \n* [on filming _Nine (2009)_ (qv)] My favorite memories are \'Sophia Loren\' (qv) holding my four week-old baby, because that is something that goes down for me in the great memories, and also becomes part of Sunday Rose\'s infant picture book. I mean, you can\'t do better than that. \n* [on acting] It\'s the desire to study the human condition, the desire for collaboration, to learn and absorb, and to lead a well-examined life. \n* I work well with people who are extreme. \n* [on \'Lars von Trier\' (qv)] He does things cinematically that nobody else does. And whether you love him or hate him, the filmmaking is incredibly strong. \n* [on \'Stanley Kubrick\' (qv) and working with him on _Eyes Wide Shut (1999)_ (qv)] Stanley wanted six months of rehearsal. He didn\'t want to start, and then he didn\'t want to finish. Stanley taught Tom [Cruise] and I, \'Never say no.\' When someone proposes an idea, you never shut it down. And that\'s a good lesson that goes far beyond work. That\'s a life lesson. \n* [on considering saying no to projects that take her away from her kids and husband for too long] I\'ve worked hard to get to this place personally. I don\'t take any of it for granted. I cherish it. I\'m more than willing to make those sacrifices because, when I\'m 70 and 80, I want my family around me. I know other things can come into play about that, but it\'s certainly not going to be because I didn\'t show up. \n* I think love is the core emotion. Without that, and I\'ve certainly existed without that, it\'s a very empty life. When I won the Oscar [for \"The Hours\"], I went home and I didn\'t have that in my life. That was the most intensely lonely moment in my life. [During] my professional highs, I\'ve often had personal lows. It\'s always aggravated me that it\'s gone that way. \n* There was that whole time from when we took _Moulin Rouge! (2001)_ (qv) to the Cannes Film Festival and then all the way through to winning the Oscar for _The Hours (2002)_ (qv), that was a very strange time in my life because it was the collision of professional success and personal failure and that\'s just a very strange thing. When I won the Oscar it was kind of a mix of popping open a bottle of champagne but at the same time feeling incredibly lonely. It\'s been professional highs and personal lows and they collide. I\'m hoping one day that ­won\'t happen. Now is a good time personally but it\'s not a professional high. The highs are pretty extreme and they don\'t happen that often, do they? I much prefer the personal high anyway. I\'d never give that up. \n* I\'ve turned things down, definitely. I was pregnant and I was going to do _The Reader (2008)_ (qv) and I had to call and say, \'I\'ve spent my whole life wanting to be pregnant and I\'m finally pregnant and I won\'t be able to do it.\' And they were like, \'You could work pregnant.\' No. That\'s not what I wanted to do. \"I didn\'t want to be working while I was pregnant. Some women could, maybe if they\'d already had a lot of children, but for me it was such a huge place to be and I wanted to carry my baby and keep her healthy and peaceful. I was like, \'I\'m going to do everything I can to just bring this baby into the world. \n* [on the beginning of her career] I had huge expectations. You start out with big dreams and I mean, big dreams artistically. You want to work with the greatest living directors, make a great movie. I wanted to make a great love story, I wanted to make a great epic and then you realize that the truth of it is that it\'s so hard to make a great film. It\'s hard to get a great role. Those big expectations change to realism pretty quickly. But what\'s never changed is my desire to work with great directors and to find projects that push me out of my comfort zone and keep me alive. I still don\'t think I\'ve done my best work \n* [on her preparation for _Paddington (2014)_ (qv)] I\'m about to go down to the museum and do a taxidermy class on Saturday night. And I love animals! I also have to throw knives, so I\'ll be doing that in the backyard. I\'ve got a guy coming over to teach me how to throw a knife. I want to be able to just do it with a bit of a flourish. I believe this is my path, so I\'ll go down in fire if I have to. \n* I think when you go into the orbit of a director, they bring certain things out in you. The greatest thing you can offer is abandonment, in terms of a performance, and also deep loyalty. Because when there is enormous loyalty to them, then it\'s a very safe place. It\'s like, O.K., so she\'ll stand by me no matter what, no matter if the film even succeeds or fails. We\'re in it together, and this is ours, together, forever. Which is why I probably stay very, very close to a lot of the directors that I\'ve worked with, whether the outcome is successful or not. Because I get deeply sort of connected and close to them. Jane, Alejandro, Stephen, Lars - all of them. That\'s important to me, and the longevity of it. \n* [on choosing \'Aaron Eckhart\' (qv)]: I\'ve known Aaron for a long time and he has a side of him which is very raw and emotional, which he doesn\'t show on screen very often. \n* [on her villainous character in _The Golden Compass (2007)_ (qv)]: The beauty of this story is that it\'s a trilogy and I think that if we get to make the second and the third film - which I would love - you get to see all of the layers peel away from this woman. So that was what I was trying to do in this film, to give her the layers. There\'s a mystery there and you\'re not quite sure and you feel the texture of her. \n* Snuggling is the secret to happiness. \n* [on her West End stage play \"Photograph 51\"] It has made me fall in love with acting. The great thing about the play is I get to do it from beginning to end. Nothing ends up on the editing floor, it doesn\'t get changed. Once the actors step onto the stage, it\'s ours for 95 minutes and that\'s exquisite. Right now I\'m having a love affair with being on stage. \n* [on producing] It\'s allowed me to shape my career in terms of being able to find things that I may not get offered, that I wouldn\'t get the opportunity for. (February 2017). \n* I\'d really like to make some movie that penetrates the psyche of the world, deeply. I haven\'t done that for a long time because I tend to do smaller, more idiosyncratic films, which I love doing. I\'m working with \'John Cameron Mitchell\' (qv) again on _How to Talk to Girls at Parties (2017)_ (qv), and that\'s very idiosyncratic. Probably _Moulin Rouge! (2001)_ (qv) was the closest in the sense that I still meet people who saw it and when they talk to me they still call me Satine. So, yes, I have a lot of young gay men who come up to me and a lot of girls who come up me and I get many big hugs and they hold onto me and they say \"Oh, Satine. I love you, Satine.\" It\'s very sweet of them. (May 2016) \n* [on her West End stage play _Photograph 51\"] It was probably the most satisfying experience I\'ve ever had. In my curtain call, tears would come down my face. Being on stage like that, it\'s incredibly direct. \n* The truth of it is I love auteurs and I love philosophical filmmakers. That\'s why I love that _\"Big Little Lies\" (2017)_ (qv) got made. That\'s why I\'ve gone back to a very subversive film with _The Killing of a Sacred Deer (2017)_ (qv). I like the high-wire act of it. I believe in not towing the line. Conformity is what I run from. (February 2017). \n* I\'ve had extraordinary relationships with directors where my opinion is heard, loudly. And they were incredibly collaborative with me. But I believe in the hierarchy of the director. It\'s their art form and I\'m only there to help them achieve it. \n* The reason why I love Aquaman is \'James Wan (I)\' (qv) is an Australian, and I\'ve followed his career since he started. He\'s a really good friend of mine, and he offered to let me play Queen Atlanna. As soon as he said I could wear mother-of-pearl and be a mermaid warrior, I said I\'m done. Please. If there is one thing I have to do in my life, I have to be that. [Laughs] Because, you\'ve got to have some fun. (February 2017) \n* [on _\"Big Little Lies\" (2017)_ (qv)] People really want to talk to me about it and really analyze it. It\'s an amazing reaction because I\'m usually either in very avant-garde art films, or unusual films, or things that are very, very divisive. To be in something that is so accessible and still topical is very interesting to me. And to be in people\'s living rooms and be a part of their lives every week for a period of time, I like that. It\'s a very invigorating and stimulating place to be as an actor. (March 2017) \n* I\'m about to turn 50 in two weeks and I would never have thought this is gonna be one of the best years of my life. So many things has happened to me creatively. When I was about 40 I remember thinking \"Oh, at 50 I\'ll be retiring\". But no way. So I wanna tell all the women out there, it is not over at 40, it begins, and it even begins more at 50. \n\n* _Nicole Kidman: An American Cinematheque Tribute (2003) (TV)_ (qv) \n\n* _\"South Park\" (1997) {Trapped in the Closet (#9.12)}_ (qv) \n\n* _Practical Magic (1998)_ (qv) -> $6,000,000 \n* _Moulin Rouge! (2001)_ (qv) -> $7,000,000 \n* _The Hours (2002)_ (qv) -> $7,500,000 \n* _The Stepford Wives (2004)_ (qv) -> $15,000,000 \n* _Cold Mountain (2003)_ (qv) -> $15,000,000 \n* _Bewitched (2005)_ (qv) -> $17,500,000 \n* _The Interpreter (2005)_ (qv) -> $15,000,000 \n* _Billy Bathgate (1991)_ (qv) -> $200,000 \n* _Days of Thunder (1990)_ (qv) -> $200,000 \n* _Far and Away (1992)_ (qv) -> $250,000 \n* _My Life (1993)_ (qv) -> $500,000 \n* _To Die For (1995)_ (qv) -> $2,000,000 \n* _Batman Forever (1995)_ (qv) -> $2,500,000 \n* _The Portrait of a Lady (1996)_ (qv) -> $2,500,000 \n* _The Peacemaker (1997)_ (qv) -> $5,000,000 \n* _Eyes Wide Shut (1999)_ (qv) -> $6,500,000 \n* _Birthday Girl (2001)_ (qv) -> $1,500,000 \n* _The Others (2001)_ (qv) -> $7,000,000 \n* _Dogville (2003)_ (qv) -> $2,800,000 \n* _Birth (2004)_ (qv) -> $3,000,000 \n* _The Invasion (2007/I)_ (qv) -> $16,000,000 \n* _The Golden Compass (2007)_ (qv) -> $ 15,000,000 \n* _Margot at the Wedding (2007)_ (qv) -> $ 6,000 \n* _The Invasion (2007/I)_ (qv) -> $16,000,000+ \n* _Australia (2008)_ (qv) -> $13,000,000 \n\n* \"W\" (USA), May 2012, Vol. 41, Iss. 5, pg. 99, by: Lynn Hirschberg, \"An Affair to Remember\" \n* \"The Independent Arts & Books Review\" (UK), 5 December 2008, Iss. 6909, pg. 4 - 6, by: Elaine Lipworth, \"\'I\'ve been through a lot in my life\'\" \n* \"Ladies Home Journal\" (USA), October 2007, Vol. 124, Iss. 10, pg. 120-126, by: Jeanne Marie Laskas, \"Nicole Kidman\'s Inner Circle\" \n* \"Parade\" (USA), 17 December 2006, pg. 14, by: Jeanne Wolf, \"My Favorite Holiday Memory\" \n* \"Widescreen\" (Germany), 6 July 2005, Iss. 08/2005, pg. 54, by: Dörte Langwald, \"Ich sehe mich nicht als großen Star.\" \n* \"Playboy\" (USA), February 2005, Vol. 52, Iss. 2, pg. 53-58+132, by: Stephen Rebello, \"Playboy Interview: Nicole Kidman\" \n* \"Independent\" (UK), 5 November 2004, by: Leslie Felperin, \"Nicole Kidman: Born to be a blank canvas\" \n* \"The Advocate\" (USA), 25 May 2004, Iss. 915, pg. 42-47, by: Alonso Duralde, \"\"Welcome to Summer Camp\"\" \n* \"Widescreen\" (Germany), 12 March 2003, Iss. 04/2003, pg. 44-45, by: Karen Martin/Silke Tittel, \"Ich mochte dieses klassische Profil\" \n* \"Vimadonna\" (Greece), 23 February 2003, Iss. 12, pg. 148-153, by: Yannis Zouboulakis, \"Pios fovate tin Nicole Kidman\" \n* \"Vanity Fair\" (USA), December 2002, Iss. 508, pg. 322-329, by: Ingrid Sischy, \"Nicole\'s New Light\" \n* \"Glossy\" (Netherlands), December 2002, Iss. 13, pg. 68-70, by: BANG, \"\'Voorlopig geen nieuwe liefde voor mij\'\" \n* \"Humo\" (Belgium), 9 October 2001, Iss. 3188, pg. 164-165+167, by: Michel Rebichon, \"\'Moulin Rouge\': konijn met pluimen dan maar?\" \n* \"Red\" (UK), October 2001 \n* \"Preview \" (The Netherlands), September/October 2001, Iss. 7, pg. 10, by: Hylke Brandsma, \"Moulin Rouge: Kidman over McGregor, muziek & haar moeder\" \n* \"Urban Cinefile\" (Australia), 7 June 2001, Vol. n/a, Iss. n/a, pg. n/a, by: Jenny Cooney Carrillo, \"Best Times, Worst Times\" \n* \"Beau Monde\" (Hungary), June 2001, Vol. 2, Iss. 6, pg. 18-21, by: Anikó Návai, \"Képes vagyok a szerelemre\" \n* \"Hello!\" (UK), 15 May 2001, Iss. 662, pg. 78-80, by: Isabelle Caron, \"Nicole Kidman\" \n* \"Talk\" (USA), September 2000, Vol. 2, Iss. 1, pg. 124-129+184- 185, by: Nell Campbell, \"The Pride of Australia\" \n* \"Cinema\" (Hungary), June 2000, Iss. 103, pg. 90-91, by: Scott Orlin, \"Nemet mondjak?\" \n* \"Urban Cinefile\" (Australia), 2000, Vol. na, Iss. na, pg. na, by: Sue Vermillions, \"Kidman, Nicole\" \n* \"Cinema\" (Greece), October 1999, Iss. 105, pg. 75-77, by: Loukas Katsikas, \"Stin skia dio gigandon\" \n* \"Cood Housekeeping\" (USA), July 1999, by: Liz Smith \n* \"Premire\" (USA), April 1999 \n* \"Star inc.\" (Canada), March 1999, Vol. 1, Iss. 3, pg. 42-47, by: Steven Hardy, \"Sortir de l\'ombre de Tom Cruise\" \n* \"Movieline\" (USA), October 1998, Vol. 10, Iss. 2, pg. 2+50-52+57-60+92-94, by: Lawrence Grobel, \"Nic at Twilight\" \n* \"Evening Standard Hot Tickets\" (UK), 3 September 1998, pg. 4+5, by: Marianne Gray, \"West End girl\" \n* \"McCall\'s\" (USA), May 1998, by: Gary Socol \n* \"Vanity Fair\" (UK), November 1997, by: Michael Shnayerson \n* \"Cleo\" (Australia), March 1997, \"Kidmans Candour\" \n* \"Good Weekend\" (Australia), 1 February 1997, by: Anne Summers \n* \"Premiere\" (UK), 1997, Vol. 5, Iss. 2, pg. 44-51, by: Matt Mueller, \"Kidman forever\" \n* \"Us\" (USA), December 1996, Iss. 227, pg. 52-62+128-129+132, by: Margy Rochlin \n* \"Premiere\" (USA), November 1996, by: \"The Kidman Cometh\" \n* \"Premiere\" (USA), November 1996, Vol. 10, Iss. 3, pg. 78-84+120, by: Matt Mueller, \"The Kidman Cometh\" \n* \"Dolly\" (Australia), March 1996, \"Slick Chick Nic\" \n* \"Juice Magazine\" (Australia), March 1996, by: Rachelle Unreich \n* \"TV Filmes\" (Portugal), 1996, Iss. 5, pg. 15-17, by: Avik Gilboa \n* \"Rolling Stone\" (Australia), 1996 \n* \"Empire\" (UK), November 1995, Iss. 77, pg. 85-86, by: Mark Salisbury \n* \"Vanity Fair\" (USA), July 1995, \"La Femme Nicole\" \n* \"Vogue\" (USA), February 1995, Vol. 185, Iss. 2, pg. 202-214, by: Jamie Diamond, \"Nicole Springs Forward\" \n* \"Empire\" (UK), 1995, Iss. 77, pg. 85-86, by: Mark Salisbury \n* \"Seventeen\" (USA), July 1992, Vol. 51, Iss. 8, pg. 98-100, by: C. Conners, \"Nicole Kidman\" \n* \"Playboy\" (USA), July 1992, Vol. 29, Iss. 7, pg. 134-138, by: David Rensin, \"20 Questions: Nicole Kidman\" \n* \"Cosmopolitan\" (USA), July 1991, by: Leo Janos \n* \"Seventeen\" (USA), August 1990, Vol. 49, Iss. 8, pg. 222-223, by: S. Colino, \"Nicole Kidman\" \n* \"Radio Times\" (UK), 14 April 1990, pg. 8, by: Garth Pearce, \"New Kidman in town\" \n* \"Rolling Stone\" (Australia), June 1989 \n* \"Cinema Papers\" (Australia), May 1989, by: Phil Noyce \n\n* \"AARP: The Magazine\" (USA), June 2017, Vol. 60, Iss. 4, pg. 72, by: Christina Ianzito, \"The Big 5-OH: Nicole Kidman\" \n* \"The Washington Post\" (USA), 7 April 2017, Vol. 140, Iss. 123, pg. C1-C2, by: Ann Hornaday, \"Why Nicole Kidman belongs in the pantheon of great actresses\" \n* \"Beverly Hills Courier\" (USA), 30 December 2016, Vol. 51, Iss. 53, pg. 18, by: Joan Mangum, \"Palm Springs International Film Society To Honor Tom Hanks, Nicole Kidman And More\" \n* \"Parade\" (USA), 5 June 2016, pg. 2, by: Walter Scott, \"Color Her Kidman\" \n* \"USA Today\" (USA), 4 May 2016, Vol. 34, Iss. 165, pg. 2D, by: Elysa Gardner, \"Kidman leaves home for \'Fang\' - Homebody/writers\' champion picks work that moves her\" \n* \"Los Angeles Times\" (USA), 12 April 2015, Vol. 134, Iss. 130, pg. E3, by: Oliver Gettell, \"MOVIES NOW: Unregal U.S. debut for \'Grace\'\" \n* \"Star Magazine\" (USA), 21 July 2014, Vol. 41, Iss. 29, pg. 34-37, by: Sarah Cordes, \"COUNTRY WIVES TELL ALL!: Sad Truth Behind Their Smiles\" \n* \"Star Magazine\" (USA), 28 April 2014, Vol. 41, Iss. 17, pg. 34-37, by: Sarah Cordes, \"Nicole & Keith: $175 Million DIVORCE SHOCKER!\" \n* \"Spits\" (Netherlands), 28 December 2009, \"Entertainment\" \n* \"The Daily Mail\" (UK), 23 October 2009, pg. 27, by: Sara Nathan, \"Kidman attacks movie violence on women\" \n* \"The Sydney Morning Herald\" (Australia), 10 January 2009, by: Bang Showbiz, \"I squirmed throu